I can help you get organized and prepare the right questions for Michael Torres so you know whether you need more evidence before the preliminary hearing. Below is a compact, lawyer-ready checklist of questions to ask plus a prioritized evidence checklist and an immediate action plan you can follow before the next court date.

Questions to ask your attorney (Michael Torres, Dowd Legal)
- Case status & next steps
  - What is our exact next court date and what will happen at the preliminary hearing (time limits, judge’s expectations, what issues are decided)?
  - Is this matter really in small claims/justice court or another division in Maricopa County, and are there any local rules I should know?
  - Do we have any deadlines (motions, disclosures, exhibit lists, witness lists, subpoenas) before the hearing or before trial?

- Evidence & necessity
  - Based on what you’ve seen so far, which pieces of evidence are essential for the preliminary hearing and which can wait until trial?
  - Do we need originals, certified copies, or will photocopies or affidavits suffice for documents (rent ledger, lease, notices, receipts)?
  - Are there specific forms of evidence the court typically rejects in these proceedings (e.g., hearsay concerns, unauthenticated records)?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Should we bring witnesses to the preliminary hearing? If so, who (property manager, neighbors, repair contractors) and how many?
  - Do we need written witness statements/affidavits before the hearing or just contact info and oral testimony?
  - If a key witness cannot attend, can we use sworn declarations or depositions, and what is the deadline to arrange that?

- Discovery & records
  - Can we compel discovery in this court (requests for documents, interrogatories, subpoenas for bank/phone/repair records)? What’s the timeline?
  - Do you recommend issuing subpoenas now for business records (bank statements, payment processor records, maintenance vendors)? How long do subpoenas take here?
  - Are there quick ways to authenticate landlord/creditor records (business records affidavits, custodian certificates)?

- Strategy & motions
  - Based on the complaint, do we have good defenses or counterclaims? Should we prepare any pre-hearing motions (motion to dismiss, motion to strike, motion for continuance)?
  - Would you recommend trying to settle or mediate before the hearing? If so, what terms would be reasonable?
  - If my status has changed (financial/job/housing), how does that affect strategy (settlement vs litigating)?

- Practicalities & court presentation
  - How should I present exhibits at the hearing (number of copies, numbering, binder, exhibit list)?
  - What do you want me to bring to the court (original lease, receipts, photos, phone records, printed timeline)?
  - How will you handle questioning and cross-examination? Do you want me to practice testimony?

Prioritized evidence checklist (bring these to your attorney and to court if possible)
Must-have (priority)
- Complaint, summons, any court filings and notices you received
- Lease or rental agreement (signed)
- Notices served (notice to quit, 5/10/30-day notices) with proof of delivery
- Payment records: rent ledger, bank statements, cancelled checks, money‑order receipts, online payment screenshots showing dates/amounts
- Any written communications with the landlord/creditor (emails, texts, messages) arranged chronologically
- Photos/videos of the property showing condition, with timestamps if possible
- Repair invoices, estimates, receipts for repairs or property damage
- Move-in/move-out condition reports or inspection reports (if applicable)
- Witness names and contact info (neighbors, contractors, property managers)

Nice-to-have (if available)
- Police or code‑enforcement reports, inspection reports showing habitability issues
- Affidavits/sworn statements from witnesses
- Records from third parties (payment processors, utility bills) proving payments or dates
- Evidence of attempts to negotiate/settle (emails or letters)
- Any prior court judgments or related filings

How to collect & preserve evidence quickly
- Export/save copies of emails and text threads (PDF or print screen with dates)
- Screenshot messages and back them up to cloud storage; preserve originals on your phone
- Date-stamp photos (phone metadata usually includes date — keep originals)
- Request business records now (bank, payment platform, contractor invoices); ask how long it will take
- Ask potential witnesses for quick signed, dated statements you can share with your lawyer

Immediate action plan (what to do before the preliminary hearing)
1. Confirm the exact hearing date/time and where it’s held; tell Michael immediately if you haven’t already.
2. Send Michael all existing documents (lease, notices, payment proof, texts, photos) in one organized file or binder. Label or provide a simple chronological timeline.
3. Ask Michael whether he needs original documents at the hearing or if copies are OK.
4. Identify and contact potential witnesses; get contact info and short written statements if possible.
5. If records are held by third parties (banks, payment platforms, repair companies), ask Michael if he wants subpoenas issued now.
6. Ask whether a continuance is advisable if critical evidence isn’t yet obtainable.
7. Prepare a 1-page timeline of events (dates & short notes) to give to Michael and bring to court.

Questions about your changed status
- Tell your lawyer exactly what you mean by “my status has changed” (employment, income, address, health, military, etc.). Then ask:
  - Does this status change affect our defenses or settlement options?
  - Should I disclose this to the court, and if so, when and how?
  - Will this status change affect my ability to pay or to comply with any court-money orders (if those are possible)?

Practical checklist for the hearing day
- Bring labeled exhibit binder with copies: one for the judge, one for opposing party, one for you/attorney
- Bring originals of key documents in case the court requests to see them
- Bring witness contact info and written statements
- Bring a short written statement of what you intend to testify to (bullet points)
- Arrive early and confirm courtroom procedures with your attorney

Offer to help
- I can draft a concise email or message you can send to Michael listing the items you’ve gathered and asking the procedural questions above. Would you like me to draft that for you?
- If you tell me the hearing date and what you mean by “status has changed,” I can tailor the questions and the evidence checklist further.

Would you like me to draft the email to Michael now (include the items you already have and the specific questions), or would you prefer a printable one‑page checklist you can bring to your meeting?

