Good — that’s a good time to get organized. Below I’ve put together a focused list of questions to ask Michael Torres, a practical evidence checklist, and step-by-step actions you can take before the preliminary hearing so you’re ready for the legal next steps.

Short clarifying question before I go into detail: is the claim about unpaid rent/nonpayment, lease breach, possession only, or something else? If you tell me that I can tailor the checklist further.

Questions to ask your attorney (Michael Torres)
- Case status and next steps
  - What is the purpose of the preliminary hearing in this specific case (scheduling, evidence hearing, settlement conference, etc.)?
  - What should I expect to happen at the next court date?
  - Is the case likely to remain in small claims or move to a different division if certain relief is sought?
- Evidence and whether you need more
  - Based on what you already have, do you need to collect additional evidence before the next hearing? If so, what specifically?
  - Which of my documents will be most persuasive to the judge, and which are unnecessary?
  - Are there any deadlines for exchanging evidence, disclosures, or filings before the preliminary hearing?
- Witnesses and subpoenas
  - Should I have witnesses available at the preliminary hearing or only at trial?
  - Do we need to issue subpoenas for any witnesses, and if so, who and by when?
  - Do any witnesses need sworn affidavits now?
- Authentication and admissibility
  - How should I authenticate text messages, emails, payment records, photos, or video so the judge accepts them?
  - Are business records affidavits (or similar) needed to admit bank statements or ledger entries without the bank present?
- Discovery and motions
  - Should we request discovery, serve interrogatories, or file motions now (e.g., motion to compel, motion in limine)?
  - If the opposing party hasn’t produced documents, should we ask the court for sanctions or a continuance?
- Settlement and strategy
  - Is settlement advisable at this stage? If so, what settlement terms should I consider acceptable?
  - What are your recommendations for trial strategy if it proceeds to trial?
- Logistics and preparation
  - What should I bring to court on the next date (how many copies of exhibits, binder format, electronic copies)?
  - Will you be the one speaking or do I need to prepare to speak on my own?
  - Do I need to attend any court-mandated classes or mediation beforehand?
- Costs and timeline
  - What are the likely costs (court fees, subpoena fees, expert witness) and who pays them?
  - What is the expected timeline from preliminary hearing to trial if the case proceeds?

Evidence checklist to gather now (prioritize items you don’t already have)
- Lease and related documents
  - Signed lease/agreement and any written amendments or addenda
  - Move-in/move-out inspection reports, inventory lists, condition reports
- Payment records
  - Rent ledger or payment history from landlord (if available)
  - Receipts, money orders, canceled checks, bank statements showing payments
  - Screenshots or records of online rent payments, Venmo/PayPal transfers
- Notices and service
  - Any pay-or-quit, termination, or eviction notices and proof of service (signed certified mail receipts, process server affidavit)
  - Any court filings you already received (complaint/summons) and all dates served
- Communications
  - All emails, text messages, direct messages, and voicemails between you and the landlord/creditor (include date/time)
  - Notes from phone calls with dates, times, and summary of content
- Photographic/video evidence
  - Photos or video of the property condition with timestamps (before/after if relevant)
  - Photos of repairs, damages, appliances, mold, pest problems, or anything at issue
- Repairs and habitability
  - Maintenance/repair requests and responses (work orders, emails)
  - Receipts and invoices for repairs you paid for
  - City/County/health code complaints or inspection reports, if any
- Third-party evidence
  - Statements or contact information of witnesses (neighbors, contractors, property manager)
  - Police reports or 911 calls if any incident occurred
  - Any receipts for alternate housing or expenses caused by the issue
- Administrative and legal documents
  - Prior judgments, notices, or money judgments related to the property
  - Correspondence from the creditor if the landlord assigned the debt to a third party
- Chain of custody/authentication items
  - Originals or verified copies where possible
  - If relying on electronic records, screenshots showing full threads and metadata if available

How to organize and present your evidence
- Create a timeline: one page showing key dates (lease start, missed payments, notices served, repairs requested, court dates).
- Exhibit binder: numbered exhibits, with a copy for you, your lawyer, and the court/opposing counsel if required.
- Label each exhibit (Exhibit 1 — Lease; Exhibit 2 — Rent Ledger; Exhibit 3 — Texts dated MM/DD/YYYY).
- Prepare electronic copies (PDFs) saved with exhibit numbers and a master index.
- Prepare short witness statements or affidavits summarizing what each witness will say.

Practical actions to take before the next date (suggested timeline)
- Immediately (this week)
  - Confirm with Michael the actual purpose of the preliminary hearing and any required filings.
  - Gather and scan all documents listed above. Start a chronological timeline.
  - Ask Michael whether any documents need sworn affidavits or special authentication.
- Within 7–10 days
  - Draft short witness statements and ask witnesses if they’re available to testify or to sign affidavits.
  - If you need documents from third parties (bank statements, receipts), request them now.
  - If you need to subpoena someone, request that your attorney prepare and serve subpoenas early.
- 2–3 days before the hearing
  - Finalize the exhibit binder and electronic copies; print the required number of sets.
  - Go over your testimony and the timeline with Michael; clarify who will speak in court.
  - Confirm logistics: court time, courtroom number, parking, and arrival time.

Questions on admissibility and technical issues to raise with Michael
- Will the judge accept screenshots of texts/emails or do we need certified copies?
- Do we need witness affidavits to avoid bringing witnesses in person at preliminary/postponement?
- Are any of my documents hearsay unless a witness testifies to them? How to cure that?
- Do we need an expert (e.g., property inspector) to testify about condition or damages?

Points to confirm about the opposing party (creditor)
- Has the creditor properly obtained/assigned the right to sue (assignment/chain of title for the debt)?
- Who is the proper defendant — landlord, creditor, property manager?
- Are there insurance, bankruptcy, or other parallel proceedings that affect the case?

Settlement/pretrial strategy items to discuss
- Would a settlement resolve this faster with less cost? What are realistic settlement terms?
- If settlement is not possible, what are the necessary defenses and supporting evidence?
- If you are short on evidence, is a continuance appropriate to get more documents or witnesses?

Offer to help
- If you’d like, I can:
  - Draft a concise email or list of the above questions and materials to send to Michael Torres.
  - Help you format an evidence timeline or an exhibit index for the binder.
  - Create a short witness statement template you can send to witnesses.

Next step for me if you want it
- Tell me whether you want me to draft the email to Michael Torres (and any points you want included), or tell me what documentation you already have so I can prioritize what’s missing.

If you tell me the specific nature of the claim (nonpayment, breach, possession, habitability), I’ll tailor the checklist and questions even more tightly to your situation.

