I can help you get ready. First: can you clarify what you mean by “my status has changed”? (Moved out, changed employment, changed contact info, had a health change, bankruptcy, something else?) That could change what evidence you need or whether you should assert defenses/counterclaims.

Below is a focused plan and a prioritized list of questions to ask Michael Torres, plus an evidence checklist and immediate steps to take before the preliminary hearing.

High-level goals for your call/meeting with Michael
- Confirm what the lawyer plans to present at the preliminary hearing and whether more evidence is needed.
- Identify any deadlines (discovery, exhibit exchange, filing motions, subpoenas).
- Decide whether to seek more evidence or rely on existing materials.
- Prepare a short, organized trial binder/exhibit list and witnesses.

Direct questions to ask Michael Torres (prioritize these in this order)
1. Case status & immediate deadlines
   - What is our specific objective at the preliminary hearing (procedural status, narrow issues, settlement discussion, set trial date)?
   - Are there any filing deadlines or required disclosures before that date?
2. Evidence sufficiency & gaps
   - Based on what you have so far, do we need any additional evidence to prevail at the preliminary hearing or to be ready for trial?
   - What are the weakest parts of our factual case and what documents/witnesses would strengthen them?
3. Witnesses & subpoenas
   - Do you recommend we bring witnesses at the preliminary hearing or is testimony usually reserved for trial?
   - Should we subpoena any witnesses or documents now? How long will subpoenas take to serve?
4. Documents & authenticity
   - What documents must we bring originals of, and what can be copies?
   - How should we authenticate text messages/emails/photographs so the court accepts them?
5. Legal strategy & defenses
   - Which defenses or counterclaims are most viable (nonpayment, improper notice, habitability, set-off, retention of security deposit)?
   - Should we consider filing any pretrial motions (motion to dismiss, motion to exclude evidence)?
6. Settlement considerations
   - Is settlement likely/appropriate before the hearing? If so, what terms should we try to negotiate?
7. What to expect at hearing/trial
   - How long do hearings usually run in this court? What is the procedure (time limits, order of presentation)?
   - If the case goes to trial, what additional preparation will you need from me?
8. Costs & benefits
   - What are the estimated legal costs if we proceed to trial versus attempting settlement?
9. If my “status changed”
   - Given my status change (explain once clarified), how does that affect our case or required evidence?

Evidence checklist — Must have (collect these ASAP)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages)
- Payment records: canceled checks, rent ledger, bank transfers, money order receipts
- Notices served: eviction notice, pay-or-quit, termination, certified mail receipts, proof of service
- Move-in and move-out condition reports, inventory lists
- Photographs/videos of the unit with dates/timestamps (damage, repairs, conditions)
- Communications: texts, emails, written letters between you and landlord/creditor (preserve originals and create printouts/PDFs)
- Receipts/invoices for repairs, cleaning, or expenses you paid
- Police reports or 311/code enforcement complaints (if relevant)
- Utility bills showing occupancy/usage (if relevant to status)
- Security deposit receipts and documentation (amount, deductions, communications)
- Any inspection reports, contractor estimates, or receipts for remediation

Evidence checklist — Highly useful (if available)
- Witness names and contact info and short witness statements or affidavits (roommates, neighbors, contractors)
- Photos with metadata or an affidavit confirming date/time
- Text message and email exports showing full conversation context (not just snippets)
- Bank statements corroborating payments/expenses
- Lease history or previous notices from landlord/creditor
- Any prior court filings/notices in this case

Organizing evidence for court
- Make a trial binder with: table of contents, timeline (one-page), exhibit list, copies of all exhibits (label as Exhibit 1, 2, etc.), original documents in a separate section
- Bring at least 3 sets of copies: judge, opposing party, your file (more if you have witnesses)
- Prepare a one-page timeline of events and a 3–5 bullet-point opening statement of your core facts
- Prepare short witness scripts or bullet points so witnesses give focused testimony

Practical steps to take now (before your next date)
- Preserve everything: don’t delete messages, back up emails/texts/photos to cloud and PDF print important threads
- Get signed, dated witness statements if possible (simple one-page notes are fine)
- Timestamp or notarize critical documents if you can (some courts accept affidavits)
- If you need documents from third parties (bank records, repair invoices), request them now — they can take time
- Ask your lawyer about subpoenas if needed for third-party records or witnesses
- Prepare a short written list of questions/concerns for Michael to cover at the preliminary hearing

Questions about admissibility / authentication to ask your attorney
- How do we authenticate text messages, emails, or social-media messages in this court?
- Will I need an affidavit to admit photographs or can I authenticate them in person?
- Are business records (bank statements, invoices) self-authenticating here or do we need witness testimony?

Discovery & motions to consider asking about
- Is there any discovery available in this court (requests for documents, depositions)? If so, what should we request?
- Should we file any pretrial motions to limit the opponent’s evidence or force disclosure?
- If settlement is possible, when should we start formal negotiations?

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise email you can send to Michael Torres listing the prioritized questions above.
- Create a one-page timeline template and an exhibit list template you can fill with your documents.
- Help phrase the request for subpoenas or document requests if the lawyer wants you to gather specifics.

Next step
- Tell me what specifically changed about your status (housing, employment, bankruptcy, address, etc.), and whether you’d like a draft email to Michael listing these questions and the evidence you’ve collected. I’ll draft it in a short, attorney-ready format.

