I can help you get organized for the preliminary hearing and the next steps. Below I’ve put together (A) clarifying questions to answer now, (B) a prioritized checklist of evidence to gather, (C) focused questions to ask Michael Torres at Dowd Legal about whether you need more evidence and what to do next, and (D) a short action plan you can follow before the next court date. If you want, I can also draft an email or message to Michael with these items.

A — Quick clarifying questions for me (so I can tailor this further)
- What exactly do you mean by “my status has changed”? (e.g., moved, lost job, changed contact info, disability, financial status, health, received bankruptcy filing, etc.)
- What is the next court date (preliminary hearing date)?
- Do you already have an attorney-client relationship with Michael Torres (i.e., is he already representing you)? 
- What documents/evidence do you already have gathered?
- Do you know whether this is in Small Claims, Justice Court, or another division in Maricopa County? (You said small claims; eviction/forcible detainer is often in Justice Court — good to confirm.)
- Do you want me to draft a succinct email to Michael asking these questions and attaching your evidence list?

B — Evidence checklist (prioritize these; bring originals + organized copies)
1. Lease / rental agreement (full document, any amendments)
2. Rent payment history / ledger (bank statements, canceled checks, money order receipts)
3. Notices and written communications:
   - Notice to quit/eviction notices (dates & proof of service)
   - Emails, texts, letters between you and landlord/creditor
   - Any demand letters you received or sent
4. Repair/condition evidence:
   - Photos/videos with dates showing condition of property
   - Work orders, repair requests, receipts, inspection reports
5. Proof of payments you dispute (receipts, transaction IDs)
6. Notices from the creditor/assignee and documents showing chain of ownership of the debt (if the claimant is a creditor, proof they own the debt)
7. Court filings you’ve received/served (summons, complaint, affidavit of service)
8. Witness contact info and short written statements (neighbors, contractors, property managers)
9. Correspondence with government agencies or housing authorities (complaints, inspector reports)
10. Medical records or documentation if health issues are relevant
11. Evidence of attempts to mitigate (applications for housing, communications trying to pay/arrange payment)
12. Any prior judgments, related lawsuits, or bankruptcy filings
13. Photocopies of ID and lease signatory pages
14. Timeline document: create a dated, one-page timeline of key events (serve dates, payments, notices, repairs, communications)

C — Focused questions to ask Michael Torres
Use these in a meeting, call, or email. Mark the highest-priority ones for the preliminary hearing.

Case status & strategy
- Based on the complaint and current facts, what is our short-term strategy for the preliminary hearing? (Defensive only, seek dismissal, settlement, continuance?)
- How likely is the case to go to trial, and what are the critical issues we must prove or refute?

Evidence & discovery
- Do we have enough evidence now to handle the preliminary hearing? If not, what specifically is missing?
- Is discovery available in this court (requests for documents, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas)? If so, what should we request from the opposing party and by when?
- Should we subpoena (or ask the court to compel) any documents or witnesses before the hearing? If yes, whom and when will those subpoenas be served?
- Do we need expert reports or inspection reports (e.g., property condition, habitability) to support our defenses?

Defenses, counterclaims & filings
- What affirmative defenses should we assert (e.g., improper service, failure to mitigate, breach of warranty of habitability, retaliatory eviction, payment/credit, statute of limitations)?
- Should we file a counterclaim (e.g., for wrongful eviction, failure to maintain premises, improper fees)?
- Are there any immediate motions you recommend filing before the preliminary hearing (motion to dismiss, motion to continue, request for more time to gather evidence)?

Evidence handling & presentation
- How should I organize and label exhibits for court (originals vs. copies, exhibit numbers)?
- How many copies of exhibits should I bring to the hearing and to trial? Do you want digital copies (USB, emailed) as well?
- What is the best way to authenticate photo/video evidence and text messages in this jurisdiction?

Timing & deadlines
- What are the key upcoming deadlines (responses to complaint, discovery cutoffs, motion filing deadlines, pretrial disclosures)?
- If I need more time to get evidence, what are our options for requesting a continuance and how likely is it to be granted?

Settlement & alternatives
- Do you recommend attempting settlement or mediation before the preliminary hearing? If so, how to position that negotiation?
- What settlement terms (time to move, payment plan, stay of eviction) are realistic?

Logistics & consequences
- If we lose at preliminary or trial, what are the likely real-world consequences (eviction timeline, money judgment, credit impact, wage garnishment)?
- If my personal status changed (e.g., moved, changed employment, filed bankruptcy), how does that affect our defense or the court’s remedies? Do we need to notify the court or opposing counsel?

Costs & next steps
- What are the estimated costs for additional evidence gathering (experts, inspections, subpoenas)?
- What should I have completed and delivered to you before the next hearing (and how many days before)?

D — Action plan to follow before the next court date (prioritized)
1. Confirm next hearing date and type (preliminary, status, trial setting). Tell your attorney that date and ask the court minutes if available.
2. Send Michael a short cover email with:
   - The one-page timeline of events (dates only)
   - Scanned copies of all documents you currently have (lease, notices, payments, photos)
   - A list of witnesses and their contact info
   - Note about your status change and how it could affect the case
3. Gather the evidence checklist items you’re missing and prioritize:
   - If you lack payment proof: collect bank statements, receipts, money order records
   - If you lack proof of notice/service: request proof of service records from the sheriff/process server
   - If property condition matters: take current dated photos and any inspection reports
4. Ask Michael whether to issue subpoenas or discovery; if yes, provide witness contacts and document descriptions immediately.
5. Prepare witness statements/affidavits (brief, dated) and get them signed if possible.
6. Organize physical and digital exhibit folders (label exhibits “Exhibit 1 — Lease,” etc.). Bring originals to the hearing and give copies to the court and opposing counsel if instructed.
7. If you need more time to gather evidence, decide with Michael whether to request a continuance — do this ASAP because courts favor timely requests.
8. Confirm with Michael how you will present evidence at the preliminary hearing (do you testify, will witnesses appear, do we rely on affidavits?).

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a concise email to Michael Torres summarizing the key questions and attaching a timeline and evidence list.
- Create the one-page timeline template for you to fill in.
- Turn this checklist into a printable “what to bring to court” list.

Tell me if you want the email drafted (and whether to send as formal or quick), and give me the next hearing date and the exact nature of your “status change” so I can customize further.

