Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and ready for the next court date. Below is a focused list of questions to ask Michael Torres, plus a prioritized checklist of evidence to gather, how to preserve and organize it, and suggested next actions. If you want, I can also draft an email to Michael (or a checklist you can bring to the meeting).

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case status & next steps
  - What is the exact next court event (preliminary hearing, status conference, case management)? What will happen there?
  - What deadlines should I know about between now and the next date?
  - Based on the current filings, how likely is this to go to trial? What are the main issues the court will want resolved before trial?
- Evidence & discovery
  - Do we need to exchange evidence or disclosures before the hearing? If so, what is required and when?
  - Are the normal discovery rules available in this small-claims/proceeding (e.g., requests for documents, deposition, subpoenas)? If limited, how do we obtain key evidence?
  - Which specific documents or types of proof would strengthen our position (lease, receipts, communications, payment records, notices, inspection reports, photos, video)?
  - Do you recommend obtaining expert evidence or inspection reports (e.g., property condition, habitability) before the next date? If so, who can perform them and how long will that take?
- Witnesses & testimony
  - Do we need witness statements or sworn affidavits before the hearing? Who should we subpoena if they won’t voluntarily appear?
  - How should witnesses prepare (what questions to expect, whether to bring ID, how to present testimony)?
- Authentication & admissibility
  - How should we authenticate electronic evidence (texts, emails, screenshots)? Are screenshots acceptable or do we need phone records/exported messages?
  - Are there hearsay or other admissibility concerns we should address now?
- Strategy & settlement
  - Given my changed status (I’ll explain below), does that change our strategy or settlement approach?
  - Should we attempt settlement/mediation before the preliminary hearing? If yes, is there a typical range or terms we should consider?
- Motions & procedural options
  - Do you advise any pre-hearing motions (e.g., motion to dismiss, motion for more definite statement, request for continuance)?
  - If I need more time to gather evidence, how do we request a continuance and what are the chances it will be granted?
- Costs & timeline
  - What are the likely costs of preparing additional evidence (expert, subpoenas, copies, filing fees)?
  - If the case goes to trial, what is the expected timeline and what will our trial preparation look like?
- Practicalities & communication
  - How should I send you the evidence (email, secure portal, hard copies)? Preferred file formats and labeling?
  - What do you need from me right away to prepare for the next date?

Evidence checklist — immediate priority items to collect
- Case paperwork
  - Complaint / Summons / Any court filings from the creditor
  - Proof(s) of service and any responses you’ve filed
- Lease & tenancy documents
  - Signed lease or rental agreement, amendments, receipts for security deposit
  - Move-in/move-out condition reports, photographs from move-in if available
- Payment records
  - Bank statements, cancelled checks, money order receipts, PayPal/Venmo records, ledger showing rent payments
  - Any receipts for partial payments or arrangements
- Notices & communications
  - All written communications with landlord/creditor (emails, letters)
  - Text messages, voicemails (export or screenshot with timestamps)
  - Notices you received (eviction notice, demand letter, notices to pay or quit)
- Property & habitability evidence
  - Photographs or video showing property condition, damages, repairs (timestamped if possible)
  - Repair requests (emails, dated messages), maintenance logs, contractor receipts
  - Code inspection reports, municipal complaints, or 311 logs if applicable
- Third-party records
  - Police reports, incident reports, or medical records (if relevant)
  - Receipts for repairs you arranged or paid for
- Witness info & statements
  - Contact information for witnesses (neighbors, contractors, roommates)
  - Short signed witness statements or affidavits describing what they saw/heard
- Other
  - Any notices of prior court actions, judgments, or related files
  - Insurance communications, if relevant

How to preserve and prepare evidence
- Do not delete any texts, emails, or voicemails. Back them up—export chats or take screenshots with visible timestamps.
- Create a single chronological timeline (one-page) of key events and dates to give your attorney.
- Make an exhibit list and label each document (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2…). Provide both originals (or certified copies if required) and electronic copies (PDF).
- Photograph or video the property with visible dates when possible. Keep originals of receipts and contracts.
- Obtain written witness statements or affidavits ahead of time (signed & dated).
- If you anticipate needing phone records or third-party records, ask your attorney about subpoenas early — they take time to prepare and serve.
- Keep a secure backup (cloud + local) of all files and maintain a “master folder” organized by exhibit number.

Prioritized immediate actions (what to do before the next hearing)
1. Tell Michael Torres right away about your “status change” and exactly what changed (income, address, health, employment, incarceration, military status, etc.). Ask how that affects defenses, settlement, or need for continuance.
2. Send your attorney the timeline and all current court papers (complaint, summons, notice of hearing).
3. Gather the highest-priority documents now: lease, payment records, notices, and recent communications.
4. Preserve electronic evidence (don’t delete) and export important message threads.
5. Identify potential witnesses and get contact info and short written statements.
6. Ask counsel whether any immediate discovery or subpoenas should be issued now.

Questions about your status change
- You mentioned your status has changed — is that financial (lost income), residency (moved), legal (incarcerated, on probation), health-related, or something else? Tell Michael exactly what changed and ask how it affects:
  - Your ability to attend hearings
  - Ability to pay any judgment or settlement
  - Possible defenses or eligibility for relief
  - Need to request a continuance or file any special motion (e.g., military protections)

What to bring to your meeting with Michael
- One-page timeline of events
- Copies of all court papers
- Copies of the evidence listed above (organized by exhibit)
- List of witnesses and short notes on what each will say
- List of questions you want Michael to answer (use the list above)

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email to Michael Torres summarizing the status change and asking the key questions above? (If yes, tell me any details you want included and whether to send or draft.)
- Create a printable one-page timeline and exhibit checklist you can bring to the meeting?
- Help you format/export text messages or prepare witness affidavit templates?

Tell me which of the follow-ups you'd like (or share the exact “status change” and your next-hearing date) and I’ll draft the email or checklist for you.

