Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Yes — I can help. Below is a focused, practical checklist you can use with Michael Torres to determine whether you need more evidence before the next court date and to get ready for the preliminary hearing / possible trial.

Start by telling your attorney (or asking) these basic facts so they can give specific advice:
- When exactly is the preliminary hearing (date/time/location)?
- What was the last court filing or order in the case?
- What does “your status has changed” mean (moved out, new income, disability, bankruptcy filing, changed contact info, incarceration, etc.)?
- Have you already exchanged any evidence with the opposing party or the court? If so, what?

Questions to ask Michael Torres (organized by purpose)

1) Case status & next steps
- What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing in this case (procedural scheduling, evidence, settlement, or an evidentiary hearing)?
- Given the case facts, what are the realistic next steps and timeline (possible continuance, trial, eviction enforcement)?
- If I can't attend or need more time, how do we ask for a continuance and what consequences/risk does that carry?

2) Evidence & proof
- What specific evidence do we need to prove my position (lease, rent ledger, receipts, communications, photos, repair records, inspection reports, police or code enforcement reports)?
- Which items are essential to have before the preliminary hearing, and which can wait until trial?
- Are there any gaps in my records that are likely to hurt my case? What documents should I prioritize obtaining now?
- Are any of my documents or communications potentially inadmissible or vulnerable to objection? If so, how can we fix that?

3) Witnesses & subpoenas
- Do we need live witnesses at the preliminary hearing or at trial (neighbors, maintenance staff, landlord/manager, repair contractors)?
- Should we get sworn statements/affidavits from witnesses now?
- If we need records (phone, bank, employer) or a witness who won’t cooperate, should we subpoena them now? What is the timeline to serve subpoenas in Maricopa County?

4) Changes in your status (specific implications)
- How does my changed status affect the case (ability to pay, housing status, defenses, settlement leverage)?
- If I moved out or paid partially, what proof is needed to show payment or move-out date?
- If I filed bankruptcy or am about to, how does that affect the landlord/creditor’s actions and should I notify the court/counsel immediately?

5) Discovery & exchange
- Are there discovery obligations or deadlines I need to meet before the hearing (exchanging exhibits, witness lists)?
- Do we need to request production of specific documents from the opposing party? If so, when and how?
- What is the best way to serve and preserve electronic evidence (texts, emails, photos)?

6) Strategy & settlement
- What are the likely strengths and weaknesses of my case from a judge’s perspective?
- Is settlement advisable now? What settlement terms should I consider (payment plan, move-out dates, dismissal conditions)?
- If settlement isn’t possible, what should our focus be at the preliminary hearing vs. trial?

7) Courtroom logistics & outcomes
- What should I expect at the preliminary hearing (format, length, judge’s typical approach)?
- What happens if the judge rules against me at the hearing (judgment, timeline for eviction or appeal)?
- If the creditor gets a judgment, what are the remedies they can pursue and how quickly (eviction/writ of restitution, wage garnishment, bank levy)?

Evidence checklist — prioritize these before the next date

Must-have (get these first)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages)
- Rent payment records (bank statements, cancelled checks, money order receipts, online payment receipts)
- Itemized rent ledger from landlord (if available)
- Any written notices from landlord or to landlord (eviction notice, notice to pay/quit, repair notices)
- Communications: texts, emails, letters between you and landlord or creditor
- Photos/videos (dated) showing unit condition or repairs
- Court papers: summons, complaint, any prior motions/orders

High-priority/supporting evidence
- Repair requests and responses, maintenance logs, contractor receipts
- Witness contact info and short written statements (roommate, neighbors, maintenance staff)
- Police or code enforcement reports (if they visited)
- Bank or payroll records showing financial changes
- Proof of payments made after the complaint (if applicable)

If available / helpful
- Medical or disability documentation (if it explains inability to pay or need for accommodations)
- Any prior settlement offers or agreements
- Housing agency/tenant advocacy communications
- Photocopies of IDs and matching addresses for proof of occupancy

How to prepare and present evidence
- Create a simple timeline/chronology of events (date → short note) and attach key exhibits by date.
- Label exhibits (Exhibit A, B, C) and bring at least three copies (one for judge, one for opposing counsel, one for you/attorney).
- Prepare a 1-page “case summary” you can give your attorney and the judge summarizing the facts and what you want the court to do.
- Bring originals and organized copies; scan and send copies to your attorney in advance.

Practical questions about admissibility & proof
- Will hearsay objections be a problem for messages or statements? If so, which documents do we need witness testimony for?
- Are there affidavits or declarations we should prepare and file in advance?
- Do we need to pre-mark exhibits with the court rules in Maricopa County?

If you need documents from third parties
- Ask your attorney to issue subpoenas early (bank records, phone records, employer records) — there’s usually lead time.
- Ask whether any records requests require a court order or protective language.

Sample short email/script you can send Michael (copy/paste)
Hello Michael — I have the preliminary hearing on [date]. I want to be prepared. Could you please advise:
1) What evidence you think is essential before the hearing and what can wait for trial?
2) Whether we should subpoena any records or witnesses now (if so, which)?
3) Whether my recent status change (I [briefly state change]) affects our strategy or the documents I should get?
4) Any exchange/discovery deadlines, and whether you want me to prepare a timeline and labeled exhibits for you to file/send to the court?
Thanks — I can gather documents and send them to you today/tomorrow.

Next actions I recommend you do now
- Tell me (or Michael) what you mean by your “status has changed.” That impacts what evidence matters most.
- Gather the “must-have” documents and make a checklist of what you already have vs. what’s missing.
- Decide whether you want me to draft the email above into a full message to Michael or help organize your exhibit binder.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a fuller email to Michael including the case specifics and the checklist,
- Create a printable exhibit checklist and timeline template you can fill in,
- Or help prioritize which documents to collect first once you tell me what you already have and what changed.

Note: I’m not your lawyer. Use this checklist to guide your conversation with Michael Torres — he can tell you exactly what is required under Arizona and Maricopa County rules. Would you like me to draft the email to Michael or make a timeline template for you to fill in?

