Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this checklist will make your meeting with Michael Torres productive and help you decide whether you need to gather more evidence before the next court date. First: can you tell me what you meant by “my status has changed”? (Examples that matter: you moved out, you paid rent, you filed bankruptcy, you started receiving public assistance, you have a new address, you got a new job, health changed, criminal charge, etc.) That detail will let me prioritize what to collect and what procedural protections to ask about.

Below is a practical list you can bring to your attorney or use to email/call them.

1) Immediate questions to ask your attorney (prioritize these before the next date)
- Which court exactly is handling this — small claims, justice court, or another court in Maricopa County? What are the specific local rules and deadlines we must follow?
- What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing (settlement, scheduling, evidence exchange, timeline to trial)?
- Given my changed status, does that affect my defense/exposure or require filing anything with the court before the next date?
- Do we need to exchange (disclose) evidence with the opposing party before the next hearing? If so, what must we produce and by when?
- Are there any upcoming deadlines for discovery, witness lists, motions, subpoenas, or trial exhibits I should know about?
- Do you recommend collecting additional evidence now? If yes, which items are highest priority?
- If I can’t get additional evidence before the next date, can we ask for a continuance? What are the likelihood and procedure for that?
- What is our best immediate strategy (settle, prepare to contest, file a particular motion)?
- If the case goes to trial, what is the expected timeline, likely witnesses, and estimated costs?
- Who will represent me at the preliminary hearing? Will you appear in person or via phone/video?

2) Evidence/documents to gather now (bring originals + copies; electronic is fine if readable)
- Lease or rental agreement (including any amendments)
- Rent payment ledger/history (bank statements, canceled checks, money order receipts, Venmo/PayPal screenshots)
- Any written notices served or received (eviction notice, 5-day/10-day notices, breach notices) and proof of service (certified mail receipts, process server confirmations)
- All communications with landlord/creditor/property manager (texts, emails, letters, voicemails — preserve originals and screenshots with timestamps)
- Move-in/move-out condition reports and dated photos/videos of the property
- Repair requests and maintenance records, invoices, and receipts (if habitability is relevant)
- Security deposit records and any correspondence about deductions
- Any receipts or invoices showing repairs you paid
- Witness info and short statements (neighbors, roommates, contractors) — names, phone numbers, what they can attest to
- Court documents you’ve already received or filed (complaint, summons, any motions, Judge’s orders)
- Any contracts/agreements with third parties (property manager, collection agency)
- Relevant public records (DBA, business registration of landlord/creditor, if applicable)
- If you made payments to the creditor or negotiated, have copies of the agreements or payment receipts
- If you filed bankruptcy or have a pending case, proof of filing and bankruptcy docket number

3) Questions about evidence handling and admissibility
- Which documents will be admissible at the preliminary hearing vs at trial?
- How should exhibits be labeled and organized for court (format, number of copies)?
- Do we need original documents, or are certified copies/photo copies OK?
- How do we authenticate electronic evidence (texts, emails)? Do we need witnesses to corroborate?
- Should we get written witness statements or affidavits now?

4) Discovery, subpoenas, and third-party evidence
- Can we subpoena records from banks, payment platforms, property managers, or municipal departments if needed? Deadline for subpoenas?
- Is written discovery allowed in this court (requests for documents, requests for admission, interrogatories)? Timeline?
- If a witness needs to appear, what’s the procedure and timeline to issue a subpoena?

5) Motions and procedural options to discuss
- Are there procedural motions you want to consider before the next hearing (motion to dismiss, motion to compel, motion for continuance, motion for summary judgment)?
- Are there defenses I should assert in writing now?
- Should we consider filing a counterclaim or setoff?

6) Settlement and alternatives
- Is settlement likely or advisable? What are acceptable settlement terms you should consider?
- If mediation is available, when can it happen and would you recommend it?
- If we settle, what paperwork will need to be filed to protect me (release, dismissal, payment plan in writing)?

7) Practical logistics and cost questions
- What will you need from me right away? (List specific documents or actions.)
- How much time will you need to prepare for the preliminary hearing or trial?
- Estimated legal fees and additional costs (process servers, deposition fees, subpoena costs).
- How will we communicate and how quickly can I expect responses?

8) If you suspect new legal consequences (bankruptcy, criminal, etc.)
- Ask whether my changed status (describe it) creates any immediate court filing obligations or requires notifying the court or opposing party.
- If you’ve filed bankruptcy, ask how an automatic stay affects the eviction/collection action and whether we need to file anything with the court.

9) Day-of-hearing checklist (what to bring)
- Copies of all exhibits, organized and numbered
- Binder with timeline of events and key documents tabbed
- Short witness notes (who says what, approx. time needed)
- Court paperwork and attorney contact info
- Proof of identity and address (if needed)

10) Sample short script/email to send to Michael Torres
Hi Michael — I have a preliminary hearing coming up in the Maricopa County case (landlord-tenant / creditor v. me). My situation has changed (briefly state the change). Can we confirm: which court is handling this, what the preliminary hearing will cover, and whether we need to exchange evidence beforehand? I can bring [list top 3 documents you have]. Please let me know any other documents I should collect now and whether we should request a continuance. I’m available [give 2–3 times] to talk. Thanks, [Your name]

If you want, I can draft a longer email for you with more context or a phone script for the call. If you tell me what “status changed” means, I’ll tailor the questions and prioritize which documents are most urgent to collect.

