I can help you get organized and ready for the preliminary hearing. Below are focused questions to ask Michael Torres plus a practical evidence checklist and steps to preserve & present what matters to the court. You can use this directly in your meeting or I can draft a short email to Michael with these items — tell me which you prefer.

Questions to ask your attorney
- Case basics & logistics
  - What is the court date, time, room, judge, and case number for the preliminary hearing and any upcoming deadlines I must meet?
  - Will you represent me at the preliminary hearing and at trial if it goes that far? If not, what should I expect?
  - What typically happens at a Maricopa County small claims preliminary hearing in a landlord-tenant/eviction-related matter?

- Evidence & timeline
  - Based on the complaint, what elements does the creditor (plaintiff) need to prove, and which facts do I need to rebut?
  - Do we need more evidence before the preliminary hearing, or is this mainly an initial scheduling/status conference?
  - Are there any court deadlines for exchanging exhibits, witness lists, or pretrial disclosures? If so, what are they and how should we meet them?
  - Do you recommend filing any motions before the preliminary hearing (motion to dismiss, motion for more definite statement, motion to compel, etc.)?

- Specific documents & records
  - Which documents are essential for my defense or counterclaim (lease, payment history, notices, repair requests, communications, receipts, photos)? Anything extra that would be helpful?
  - Do you want originals, certified copies, or PDFs? How many copies and where should they be sent/filed?
  - Should we subpoena any records (bank statements, payment processor records, business/creditor records) or request records through discovery now?

- Witnesses & statements
  - Which witnesses would be helpful (neighbors, property managers, repair persons)? Should we collect sworn affidavits now?
  - Do we need to subpoena witnesses for the hearing or wait until trial?

- Strategy & outcomes
  - Based on my current status change, how could that affect strategy or potential defenses (e.g., payment attempts, habitability, improper notice, service issues)?
  - Are there potential counterclaims or offsets I should raise (security deposit, repairs, breach of warranty of habitability, unlawful practices)?
  - What are realistic outcomes (dismissal, settlement, judgment amount, payment plan) and your recommended settlement approach?

- Practical & cost considerations
  - What evidence will you personally need from me before the hearing and by when?
  - What are probable costs and timeline if we proceed to trial?
  - If I want to attempt settlement, what settlement range is reasonable and who should initiate negotiations?

Evidence checklist — what to gather now
- Lease/contract documents
  - Original lease, any written amendments, signed move-in/move-out inspection forms.

- Payment records
  - Bank statements, cancelled checks, money order receipts, online payment receipts, rent ledgers showing dates/amounts, proof of partial payments, returned-check notices.

- Notices & service documents
  - Any notices to pay or quit, eviction notices, certified mail receipts, proof of service (who served, when, how).

- Communications
  - Texts, emails, letters, voicemail transcriptions, maintenance requests, landlord/creditor messages. Save screenshots and original files (don’t delete).
  - If messages are from third-party platforms (Zelle, Venmo, PayPal), get transaction details and receipts.

- Property condition / habitability
  - Photos or videos with dates showing unit condition, repairs needed or completed.
  - Repair invoices, contractor estimates, work orders, city code complaints or violation records.

- Third-party records
  - Utility bills, occupancy records, police reports (if relevant), municipal code complaints, building inspection reports.

- Court & case documents
  - Complaint, summons, any prior court filings, prior orders, notices from the court, any correspondence from opposing counsel.

- Witness info and statements
  - Names, contact info, short written summaries of what each witness can say; consider notarized affidavits for key witnesses.

- Chronology / timeline
  - One-page timeline of key events (dates of nonpayment, notices, communications, repairs, court filings). Put exhibits by date and label them.

How to preserve evidence
- Make digital backups immediately (PDFs of emails/texts/screenshots). Do not delete original messages.
- Print and keep originals where possible (checks, receipts). If originals are electronic, print and retain files with metadata if possible.
- Take dated photos and videos; note when and where they were taken.
- Ask witnesses for short written statements (signed) or sworn affidavits if possible.
- Keep a secure folder (physical binder and a thumb drive/cloud folder) with numbered exhibits and a contents page.

How to present evidence at court
- Prepare an exhibit list with exhibit numbers and short descriptions.
- Make copies for: the judge, opposing counsel, and your attorney (and keep the originals). Typically 3 copies is safe.
- Organize exhibits chronologically and tabbed in a binder; include the timeline as the first tab.
- Ask your attorney how they prefer to receive files (PDFs, email, shared drive) and the format for exhibits.

Quick checklist to bring to your attorney right now
- Case number and court notice
- Copy of complaint and all court papers
- Lease and payment records for the period in dispute
- All communications with the creditor/landlord (texts, emails)
- Photos/videos of unit condition or repairs
- Names and contact info for potential witnesses
- Any notices or service receipts received

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise email to Michael Torres listing these questions and asking what he needs from you before the preliminary hearing.
- Create a printable evidence binder template and exhibit list you can fill in.
- Help you convert texts/emails into a clean chronological timeline or sample affidavit template for a witness.

Would you like me to draft the email to Michael Torres now (short and professional) or build the evidence binder template for you? If yes, tell me which option and any details you want included (hearing date, specific status change, items you already have).

