I can help you get organized for the preliminary hearing and decide whether you need more evidence. Below is a practical, prioritized checklist you can bring to your meeting with Michael Torres (Dowd Legal), plus a set of clear questions to ask him so you can confirm next steps and deadlines.

First — one quick question
- When you say “my status has changed,” can you briefly say what changed? (e.g., you moved out, your income changed, you filed bankruptcy, your contact info changed, you’re no longer living at the property, etc.) That can change what evidence you’ll need.

Immediate checklist — gather these right away (hardest to replace later)
- Lease/rental agreement (all pages, any addenda)
- Notices (eviction notice, notice to pay or quit, notice to vacate) and any certified mail/receipt records
- Rent payment records (bank transfers, checks, payment platform history, receipts)
- Communication with landlord/creditor: texts, emails, letters, voicemails, messenger threads (save screenshots with dates)
- Photographs/videos of the unit (date/time if possible) showing condition, repairs, or move-out status
- Repair/maintenance requests and responses (work orders, emails to property manager)
- Receipts, invoices, estimates for repairs you paid or evidence landlord paid or didn’t pay
- Security deposit records (receipt, itemized deductions, move-out statement)
- Witness contact info and brief written statements (neighbors, co-tenants, property manager) — get short signed/dated statements if possible
- Any government/code inspection reports or citations for property conditions
- Bank statements showing deposits/withdrawals related to rent or damages
- Receipts or proof of alternative housing costs if you were forced to move or otherwise incurred damages

Questions to ask Michael Torres (grouped and ready to use)
- Case status and representation
  - Am I currently represented by you for the preliminary hearing and trial? If so, what will you handle and what do you need from me?
  - Will you appear at the preliminary hearing? If not, what should I expect and what should I bring?

- Evidence & priority
  - Based on the facts of my case, which pieces of evidence matter most? What should I collect immediately?
  - Are there any documents that, if missing, would significantly weaken my position?
  - Which evidence is hardest to obtain later that I should prioritize now?

- Procedural deadlines and disclosures
  - What are the key upcoming deadlines (preliminary hearing date, discovery deadlines, exhibit/witness lists)?
  - Do I need to exchange exhibits/witness lists with the other side before the hearing/trial in Maricopa County? If so, by when and in what format?
  - Are there local court rules (Justice Court / Small Claims / Eviction procedures) I should know about for evidence or hearing procedure?

- Witnesses & subpoenas
  - Which witnesses should I plan to have? Should we get written witness statements or sworn affidavits now?
  - If a witness is reluctant, can we subpoena them? How long does that process take here?

- Authentication & admissibility
  - How should I authenticate digital evidence (text messages, emails, payment screenshots) so the court will accept them?
  - Are there common evidence objections in these cases I should know about?

- Discovery & investigations
  - Is formal discovery available/applicable in this type of case in this court? If so, should we serve discovery requests now?
  - Do we need any expert evidence (e.g., for habitability or repair costs)?

- Strategy & defenses
  - Based on what I’ve told you, what defenses or counterclaims should we be prepared to present (improper notice, breach of warranty of habitability, failure to mitigate, etc.)?
  - Would you recommend trying to settle or mediate before the next hearing? If so, what settlement range would be reasonable?

- Logistics & next steps
  - What do you want me to bring to the preliminary hearing (documents, copies for the judge/opposing party, witnesses)?
  - Do you want me to prepare a timeline of events? If so, what format is most helpful?
  - How soon do you need the documents I’m gathering? How should I deliver them to you (email, secure upload, in-person)?

- Costs & practical questions
  - What costs should I expect for subpoenas, expert reports, or additional filings?
  - If I miss gathering an item now, can it still be introduced later at trial? What are the risks?

How to prioritize evidence collection (practical plan)
1. Right now (next 48–72 hours): save and back up all digital communications and payment records; take dated photos of the property if applicable; get signed short witness statements.
2. Within 1 week: collect bank statements, certified mail receipts, lease and notices, repair invoices.
3. Before next meeting/hearing: create a one-page timeline of events with dates and attach supporting docs in chronological order. Provide copies (paper or PDF) to your attorney and bring originals to the hearing.

How to present the materials to your attorney
- Make a folder (digital or physical) labeled: “Eviction — [Your Name] — [Case # if available]”
- Inside: 1-page timeline, then grouped folders: Lease/Notices, Payments, Communications, Photos, Repairs, Witnesses
- Provide a list of witnesses with short notes on what they will say and contact info

If you want, I can:
- Turn the checklist/questions into an email draft you can send to Michael Torres asking for his input and what he specifically needs. (Tell me whether you want it sent now or saved as a draft, and any tone or extra details.)
- Help format the timeline or draft short witness statement templates you can give to people.

Next step from me
Tell me (1) what exactly changed in your status, and (2) whether you want me to draft an email to Michael Torres with the checklist and questions or prepare a timeline template or witness statement template.

