I can help you get organized and ready for the next hearing. Below is a focused set of things to ask Michael Torres, and a checklist of evidence and tasks to gather before your preliminary hearing. If you want, I can also draft an email or message to Michael with these items.

1) Key facts I need from you (so I can tailor this further)
- When is the preliminary hearing date?
- Exactly what changed in your status? (e.g., you moved out, you paid rent, you filed bankruptcy, you were served, your employment changed, etc.)
- Do you already have a copy of the complaint, summons, and any filings from the creditor?
- Do you have lease, payment records, notices, photos, or messages relevant to the dispute?

2) Questions to ask your attorney (Michael Torres)
- What is the purpose of the preliminary hearing in this case and what should I expect to happen that day?
- Based on our current position, do we need additional evidence before the preliminary hearing or can we handle it later?
- What specific pieces of evidence would strengthen our case? (examples: lease, rent ledger, cancelled checks, bank statements, text messages, photos, repair invoices, inspection reports)
- Are there any imminent deadlines I must meet (discovery, exhibit exchange, filing motions)? What are the exact dates?
- Do we have to exchange documents with opposing counsel before the next hearing? If so, what must be produced and by when?
- Should we try to subpoena witnesses or documents now? If yes, who and how soon can subpoenas be issued?
- Do you recommend obtaining any expert evidence (e.g., contractor estimates, habitability inspection)?
- Are affidavits or written witness statements acceptable, or do the witnesses need to appear in person?
- What evidence will the judge likely find most persuasive in this court (small claims/justice court standards)?
- Do we need originals or are copies okay? How should documents be authenticated?
- How many copies of each exhibit should I prepare and bring to court?
- Will you represent me at the hearing, and if not, what should I prepare if I must appear pro se?
- Do you recommend offering or accepting a settlement before the hearing? What are realistic outcomes or risks?
- If my status change (e.g., bankruptcy, moved out) impacts the case, what immediate steps protect my rights? Does that trigger any stays or special procedures?
- If we intentionally delay (continuance), what effect would that have on the case timeline and our ability to gather evidence?
- What are the possible consequences if we are unprepared at the preliminary hearing?

3) Evidence checklist — gather and organize these (bring originals + copies)
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages)
- Complaint and summons (all court documents you’ve received)
- Payment records:
  - Rent ledger (landlord/creditor’s ledger if available)
  - Your bank statements showing rent payments
  - Canceled checks or payment receipts
  - Money order receipts, Venmo/PayPal records, etc.
- Notices and communications:
  - Eviction notices, 3/5/10-day notices, notice to vacate
  - Any written communications with landlord/creditor (emails/texts/letters)
  - Certified mail receipts or proof of delivery for notices you sent/received
- Photographs and videos (date-stamped if possible) showing condition of the unit, damages, repairs, etc.
- Repair records and invoices (contractors, receipts, repair requests)
- Move-in and move-out inspection reports, inventory, photos
- Witness info:
  - Names and contact info of neighbors, roommates, contractors who can testify
  - Short written statements or affidavits from witnesses (signed and dated)
- Proof of service documents (showing how you were served)
- Any prior judgments, small claims filings, or related court orders
- Text message screenshots or email threads (include metadata or printouts with timestamps)
- Timeline summary: one-page chronological summary of facts and key dates (helpful for attorney and judge)
- Anything showing payment arrangements, partial payments, or promises from landlord/creditor
- Evidence of tenant defenses (e.g., uninhabitable conditions, retaliation, improper notice, security deposit disputes)
- Documentation of any legal events that could affect the case (e.g., bankruptcy filing, active restraining order, guardianship)

4) How to organize materials for court
- Create a binder with a labeled tab for each exhibit type (Lease, Payments, Notices, Photos, Witness Statements, Court Filings)
- Number each exhibit (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2, etc.). Make an exhibit list with short descriptions and the date.
- Make at least three sets of copies: one for the judge, one for opposing counsel, one for you (plus one for your attorney if they will appear separately).
- Prepare a one-page case summary / timeline to hand the judge and your attorney.
- Keep originals in a separate envelope you can produce if requested.

5) Procedural and tactical items to confirm with Michael
- Whether the judge allows remote appearances and how to connect if necessary
- Whether Michael wants to file any pre-hearing motions (motion to dismiss, motion to compel, etc.)
- Whether to file/exchange witness lists or exhibit lists in advance
- Whether to seek a continuance if more time is needed and how to request it
- Strategy: negotiate settlement terms vs. go to trial — pros and cons specific to your case value and risks

6) Practical steps you can do now
- Make digital and printed copies of everything you have (timestamped screenshots for electronic messages)
- Write a concise timeline of events (dates + one-sentence factual notes)
- Contact any witnesses and ask if they will give a signed short statement with date
- Take clear, dated photos of the premises or items in dispute
- Locate and organize proof of payment (bank statements, canceled checks)
- Check the court’s website for local small claims / justice court rules and any forms or filing deadlines

7) Sample short message you can send Michael (I can draft a full email if you want)
Subject: Upcoming preliminary hearing — quick prep questions and documents

Hi Michael — I have a preliminary hearing scheduled on [date]. A few things changed in my status: [briefly state change]. Before we meet, can you let me know:
- What you need from me before the hearing (specific documents or witnesses)
- Any upcoming filing deadlines or required disclosures
- Whether we should subpoena anyone or seek a continuance

I’ve started collecting: lease, payment records, photos, & communication logs. Would you like me to send copies or bring originals to your office? Thanks — I want to make sure we’re ready.

8) Next step from me
- If you want, I will draft a full email to Michael with the questions above and a list of documents to attach.
- Or, upload or describe the documents you already have and tell me the exact date of the hearing and what changed in your status — I’ll create a prioritized “must-get” evidence list and a one-page timeline you can print and hand to your attorney.

Which would you like to do next?

