I can help you get organized and ready for the preliminary hearing and possible trial. Below is a focused list of questions to ask Michael Torres, a prioritized evidence checklist, practical steps to prepare before the next court date, and a short timeline you can follow. I’m not giving legal advice — this is to help you communicate clearly with your lawyer and make sure you gather what might be needed.

Key clarifying questions for you before we proceed
- When is the preliminary hearing (date)?
- What exactly “changed” about your status (moved out, income changed, eviction stayed on record, new job, illness, etc.)?
- Do you already have a copy of the lease, notices, and any prior court filings?
- Do you have any witnesses (neighbors, roommates, repair people) who will testify for you?

Questions to ask your attorney (Michael Torres)
1. Case status and strategy
- What is our current position for the preliminary hearing — defensive, settlement-focused, or procedural (e.g., ask for continuance)?
- Given my changed status, how does that affect the legal strategy or risk of judgment?
- What is the likely range of outcomes at the preliminary hearing and at trial?

2. Burden of proof & needed evidence
- What exactly must the creditor prove at the preliminary hearing and at trial? (e.g., unpaid rent amount, proper notice, right to possession)
- Do we currently have enough admissible evidence to meet the applicable standards or to defeat their claims?
- Are there specific documents or proof you think we still need to collect?

3. Evidence collection & admissibility
- Which of my documents would be most persuasive and what format is required (originals, certified copies)?
- How should I preserve and present electronic evidence (texts, emails, photos, videos)? Do we need metadata or affidavits to authenticate?
- Are business records (ledger, rent roll) or bank statements admissible as-is or do we need a custodian or a subpoena?

4. Discovery and subpoenas
- Can we subpoena documents (bank records, repair invoices, landlord receipts) or witnesses before the hearing? Is formal discovery allowed in this court?
- If I need additional documents from the creditor (ledgers, notices sent), what’s the best way to request them?

5. Motions and procedural issues
- Should we file any motions before the preliminary hearing (motion to dismiss, motion for more definite statement, motion to continue)?
- What deadlines do I need to know (exhibit exchange, witness list, filing fees)?

6. Witnesses and affidavits
- Which witnesses would help our case and how should we prepare them (affidavits, short written statements, availability for court)?
- Should any witness provide a sworn affidavit before the hearing?

7. Settlement & negotiation
- Is settlement or mediation advisable now? If so, what’s my acceptable settlement range?
- Would a continuance for more evidence hurt or help our position?

8. Trial logistics and expectations
- If the case goes to trial, how long will it take, what will the schedule look like, and what should I expect at trial?
- How many copies of exhibits do I need and to whom do I provide them at the court (judge, opposing counsel, us)?

Evidence checklist — gather and organize these now
Documentary evidence
- Lease or rental agreement (signed, including amendments)
- Rent payment ledger / receipts / money order records / canceled checks
- Bank statements showing payments (if relevant)
- Notices: notice to vacate, notice of nonpayment, any written notices sent/received (certified mail receipts)
- Court filings and summons — proof of service
- Security deposit records and move-in/move-out condition reports
- Repair invoices, estimates, receipts if repairs/disputes are at issue
- Communications: emails, texts, DMs, written letters (keep originals or screenshots with timestamps)
- Photographs / videos of property condition (with dates/time if possible)
- Police reports, code enforcement reports (if any)
- Any prior judgments or collection actions related to the tenancy

Witnesses & statements
- Contact info and short written statements or affidavits from witnesses (neighbors, roommates, repair techs)
- Expert contacts if needed (contractor estimates, property inspector)

Authentication & foundation
- Originals when possible; if only digital, preserve metadata and take dated screenshots/backups
- Keep a chain of custody log for key physical items (keys, returned property)
- If a document is from a business (ledger, repair invoice), get a business records affidavit or subpoena for the custodian if necessary

Organization & presentation
- Create an indexed exhibit binder: numbered exhibits, short exhibit list, and a one-page timeline of events
- Prepare 3 sets of exhibits for court (judge, opposing counsel, your attorney) — check the court’s preferred format
- Prepare a one-page summary for your attorney with key facts and a suggested outline of your testimony

Practical steps in the next 2 weeks (prioritized)
- Immediately: Make digital backups of all electronic communications. Don’t delete messages.
- Within 3 days: Send your attorney a folder (secure email or physical) with copies of lease, notices, payment records, and any new evidence.
- 1 week before hearing: Finalize witness list and get any witness affidavits signed and dated.
- 3–5 days before hearing: Produce exhibit binder and one-page timeline for your attorney to review.
- If more evidence is needed and you need time, ask your lawyer about filing for a continuance as soon as possible.

Questions about admissibility you can ask your lawyer
- Will text messages or Facebook messages be admitted? If so, how do we authenticate them?
- Can the creditor rely on an unsigned ledger or undocumented claims?
- Is hearsay a likely problem for certain documents and can we use affidavits to address that?

If you need to gather additional records from third parties
- Ask attorney to draft subpoenas or records requests (bank, repair company, property manager).
- If landlord claims professional accounting records exist, request certified copies or a custodian affidavit.

Settlement & cost considerations to ask your lawyer
- What are the costs and time implications of collecting additional evidence vs. accepting a settlement?
- Will collecting more evidence (subpoenas, expert) increase legal fees significantly?

Court-specific & timeline questions
- Confirm the court (small claims vs. Justice Court in Maricopa County) and any local rules or forms we must follow.
- Confirm the deadlines for exchanging exhibits or filing pre-trial forms.

What I can do to help next
- Draft a concise email to Michael Torres summarizing your changed status and asking the above priority questions (I can prepare a ready-to-send draft).
- Help you create the exhibit index and one-page timeline from the documents you provide.
- Draft witness affidavit templates or a short timeline summary for the court.

Would you like me to:
1) Draft an email to Michael Torres now (if so, tell me the preliminary hearing date and what changed in your status), or
2) Help you build the exhibit index/timeline from your documents (you can tell me what you have)?

Pick one and I’ll prepare the draft or the checklist tailored to your answers.

