2. Based on what we’ve already filed and the evidence we have, do you think we need additional evidence before the next date? If so, what specific items?
3. Which facts does the court consider most important on the preliminary hearing date and at trial (possession? rent owed? notice proper service? condition/repairs? other)?
4. Do we need witness statements or affidavits now, or can we present witnesses live at the hearing/trial?
5. What documentary evidence do you recommend I obtain and bring (see checklist below)? Are original documents required or are copies OK?
6. Are there any deadlines coming up for disclosures, evidence exchange, or motions I should know about?
7. Can we request subpoenas for witnesses or records now? If yes, which witnesses/records should we subpoena?
8. Should I preserve any specific physical evidence (photos, keys, damaged property)? How should I store it/label it?
9. If my status has changed (income, address, moved out, etc.), how will that affect our defenses or potential remedies?
10. Is settlement or mediation advisable before the preliminary hearing? If so, what are my realistic settlement options?
11. What is our strategy for the preliminary hearing — are we aiming to win on the date, postpone, or narrow the issues for trial?
12. What exhibits should I prepare and how should they be labeled/organized for the court (numbering, binder copies, exhibit list)?
13. If the court continues the case to trial, what additional evidence will we need to collect between now and trial?
14. What is the likely timeline to trial in Maricopa County for this type of case?
15. What possible procedural motions (e.g., motion to dismiss, motion for additional discovery) should we consider?
16. Are there any local court rules or filing forms specific to Maricopa County Justice Courts/small claims I need to be aware of?
17. Will you be at the next hearing, or do you want me to attend with specific information prepared?
18. How should I communicate any new evidence to you (email, portal), and what is the latest time I can give you new evidence before the hearing?

Evidence checklist — what to gather now (bring originals + 2 copies if possible)
- Lease or rental agreement (all pages, any addenda)
- Move-in/move-out inspection reports, condition inventories
- Notice(s) sent or received (e.g., notice to pay or vacate, termination notices) — include dates and proof of delivery
- Rent ledger / payment history (receipts, canceled checks, bank statements showing rent payments)
- Any written communications with landlord/creditor (emails, texts, letters) — preserve metadata (dates/times)
- Repair requests and landlord responses (texts, work orders, contractor invoices)
- Photographs and video of the property (with dates if possible) — label with file name and date
- Receipts for repairs or mitigation you paid for
- Any witness contact info and short written affidavits (roommates, neighbors, repair people)
- Court filings and proof of service (complaint, summons, answers, motions)
- Any prior judgments, notices, or related lawsuits
- Proof of your status change (pay stubs, unemployment notices, new lease, relocation documents) if relevant to defenses or damages
- Communications from the creditor (collection letters, demand letters)
- Anything showing attempts to resolve (mediation/settlement offers)
Why each matters: these items prove dates, payments, communications, property condition, and attempts to mitigate — all frequently pivotal in landlord-tenant and small claims disputes.

Practical hearing prep (logistics + presentation)
- Create a one-page timeline of key events (dates, short description) — bring copies for the judge and for the other side.
- Prepare an exhibit list (Exhibit 1 = lease, Exhibit 2 = rent ledger, etc.). Number and tab everything; bring several sets (judge, opposing counsel, your copy).
- Prepare 3–4 bullet-point opening statements: what you will prove and why the court should rule for you.
- Practice your testimony and short answers to likely questions. Keep to facts and dates.
- Bring government ID, case number, attorney contact info, and all filings.
- Arrive early and confirm courtroom / check-in procedures for Maricopa County or the specific Justice Court location.
- Ask attorney whether to bring witnesses in person or to submit sworn declarations instead.

Preservation & immediate actions
- Preserve evidence now — do not delete texts/emails. Consider taking screenshots and saving originals.
- Get written, signed witness statements if possible (short affidavits summarizing what they saw/heard).
- If you need records from third parties (bank, utility, repair companies), request them immediately — some take weeks.
- If you worry about missing a filing deadline, ask your attorney about requesting a continuance.

Questions to help evaluate whether you already have “enough” evidence
- Does what we have directly support each essential element the plaintiff must prove (or each element of your defense)?
- If the case goes to trial, what evidence would the judge likely find most persuasive?
- Are there any obvious holes in our chain of events, dates, or proofs? Which ones are high priority to fix?
- Which extra piece(s) of evidence (one or two items) would most improve our position quickly?

Possible outcomes at the preliminary hearing (so you know what to prepare for)
- Case resolved or dismissed
- Case continued/settled
- Limited rulings narrowing issues for trial (e.g., decide only on possession or damages)
- Scheduling of trial with deadlines for additional discovery/evidence

