If you want, I can then draft an email to Michael with these questions or build an exhibit list you can bring to the hearing.

A — Quick clarifying questions for you
- What do you mean by “my status has changed”? (examples: moved out, lost job/income, new address, changed immigration or disability status, bankruptcy, etc.)
- What is the exact court and hearing date/time (justice court vs. small claims — Arizona evictions are often in justice court)?
- Do you already have copies of the complaint, notices, and any communications from the creditor/landlord?
- Do you have any witnesses (neighbors, roommates, repair people) who will support your side?
Answering these lets me make the questions and checklist more specific.

B — Questions to ask Michael Torres (organized so you can read them quickly in a meeting or email)

Case status & court process
- What is the exact nature and purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing in this court? (procedural scheduling vs. evidentiary)
- Given the county and court, what rules/deadlines apply between now and trial?
- Do we need to file anything (motions, disclosures, witness/exhibit lists) before the preliminary hearing or in the days after it?

Evidence & burden
- What is the specific legal burden the creditor must meet in this eviction/small-claims matter, and what does the court typically require to meet it?
- Based on what you already know, do we currently have enough evidence to meet our goals (dismissal, settlement, defense at trial)?
- What additional evidence would most strengthen our position? Prioritize by importance and by how long it takes to obtain.

Documents to collect (logistics)
- Which documents should I gather and bring to the next hearing? (examples I already plan to collect below — ask the attorney to confirm)
- How many copies of each exhibit do I need for the judge, opposing counsel, and myself? Any preferred exhibit format or labeling?

Witnesses & testimony
- Should any witnesses attend the preliminary hearing or wait for trial?
- If we plan to call witnesses at trial, what statements or affidavits do we need now? Do we need subpoenas, and what is the timeline to issue them in Maricopa County?

Discovery, subpoenas & evidence preservation
- What discovery tools are available in this court for obtaining documents from the creditor? (written requests, subpoenas, depositions)
- How should I preserve evidence now (save original receipts, screenshots, photos, chain of custody)?

Admissibility & authentication
- Are text messages, emails, photographs, rent ledgers, bank statements, or repair/inspection reports likely to be admissible? What authentication does the court expect?
- For business records or payment records, do we need an affidavit from a custodian of records?

Motions & strategic options
- Should we consider any pre-hearing motions (motion to dismiss, motion to strike evidence, motion for continuance)?
- Is settlement or mediation advisable at this stage? If so, what are acceptable settlement terms?

If my status changed (specific to your change)
- If my status changed (e.g., loss of income, moved out, disability, bankruptcy), what legal effect does that have on defenses, procedural deadlines, or remedies in Arizona?
- Do I need to disclose this change to the court or opposing party before the hearing?

Practical & costs
- What will your recommendations and likely costs be if we collect further evidence, issue subpoenas, or prepare for trial?
- How long will it realistically take to prepare the stronger case you recommend?

C — Prioritized evidence checklist to gather now (bring originals + 3 copies; make a digital folder and a printed packet)
High priority (collect immediately)
- Complaint, any filed notices, and the court filing timestamp
- Any notices you received (eviction notice, demand letters) with dates
- Lease or rental agreement (signed pages) or any written terms of tenancy
- Proofs of payment (bank statements, cancelled checks, money order receipts, payment app records) with dates
- All communications with landlord/creditor: emails, text messages, letters, voicemail transcripts (save screenshots and originals)
- Photographs or video relevant to the dispute (dated, with context)
- Move-in/-out condition reports and any repair or inspection reports

Medium priority
- Repair requests and responses (texts, emails, work orders)
- Security deposit receipts and accounting
- Witness contact info and short summaries of what they will say
- Any correspondence showing you attempted to resolve the issue

Lower priority but useful
- Bank statements showing rent/utility payment history
- Tenant ledger or landlord’s payment records (if you can obtain them)
- Affidavits from witnesses if they can sign now
- Any relevant municipal/health code inspection reports

D — Immediate next steps (timeline for the preliminary hearing)
1. Confirm hearing purpose and date/time/location with Michael and the court clerk. Ask if it’s in Justice Court or small claims division. (If unknown, confirm now.)
2. Assemble the “high priority” documents into a numbered exhibit packet and make copies (judge + opposing + you). Prepare a single-page exhibit index.
3. Ask Michael whether to request subpoenas now and, if so, who we should subpoena and by when (subpoena lead time).
4. Preserve all electronic evidence (export emails, take screenshots with timestamps, save originals).
5. Decide whether you want to ask for a continuance now if you don’t have enough time to gather key evidence — ask Michael the procedure and likelihood of success.
6. Discuss settlement posture and whether he wants you to attempt informal resolution before the hearing.

Optional I can do for you
