Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — this is a good time to get organized. Before I give a checklist and suggested questions, two quick clarifying questions so I can tailor the list:

1) When you say “my status has changed,” what changed (employment, housing situation, financial status, address, or something else)?  
2) Do you know the exact next hearing type and date? (You said “preliminary hearing” — is that a small-claims status/scheduling hearing or an eviction hearing in Justice Court?)

If you want, I can draft a short email to Michael Torres with these questions and the evidence checklist. Tell me whether to send it, draft it as a draft, or just show it to you first.

Below is a focused list of questions to ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next date, plus an evidence checklist and practical next steps you can take now.

Questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & hearing purpose
  - What is the specific purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing (scheduling, mediation/settlement, evidence hearing, summary judgment, etc.)?
  - Is this being handled in small claims, Justice Court (eviction), or another division in Maricopa County? Are there local rules I should know about?
  - What outcome do you recommend we aim for at the preliminary hearing (continuance, settlement negotiation, get case dismissed, preserve defenses)?

- Evidence & discovery
  - What evidence do you currently have and what is missing or weak?
  - Are there evidence-exchange/discovery deadlines coming up? If so, what are they?
  - Do we need to subpoena any third-party records (bank statements, payment processor, utility records, repair records, code enforcement, etc.)? If yes, who should I identify for subpoenas?
  - Are written witness statements or affidavits sufficient, or do we need witnesses to appear live?
  - Are there chain-of-title/assignment documents for the creditor (proof they have standing to sue)? Do we need to challenge assignment authenticity?

- Documents & authentication
  - Which documents do you want me to bring to the hearing (originals vs. copies)? How many copies for the court/opposing counsel?
  - How should I organize and label exhibits so they are admissible and easy to use?
  - Do we need to authenticate texts/emails/voicemails? If so, how should I prepare them?

- Strategy & defenses
  - Based on what you know, which defenses should we prioritize (e.g., improper service, landlord failed to follow eviction procedures, payment/creditor accounting errors, breach of warranty of habitability, retaliatory eviction, statute of limitations)?
  - Should we prepare counterclaims (e.g., security deposit, damages, rent offsets) or focus on defense only?
  - Is settlement likely? If so, what settlement terms would be acceptable?

- Logistics & motions
  - If we don’t have an important piece of evidence by the hearing, what are our options (continuance, subpoena, motion to compel, stipulation)?
  - Are there any pretrial filings required (exhibit list, witness list, pretrial statement, motions in limine)? What are the deadlines?
  - Can you (or should we) file any procedural motions now (motion to dismiss, motion to strike, motion for summary judgment, motion to compel)?

- Costs & timeline
  - What is the expected timeline to trial if not settled at the preliminary hearing?
  - What are estimated costs if we proceed to trial vs. settling now?

Evidence checklist — items to gather now
- Lease/rental agreement (full document, including signatures and any addenda)
- Rent ledger/payment history (receipts, canceled checks, bank statements showing rent payments)
- Notices (eviction notices, notices to quit, pay-or-vacate, service attempts)
- Communication logs (emails, texts, letters, voicemail logs) with dates and parties
- Work orders/repair requests and responses (maintenance requests, landlord responses, code enforcement complaints)
- Photos and videos (dated, showing property condition; keep originals and copies)
- Police reports or incident reports, if any
- Any written demand letters from the creditor or their attorneys and proof of service
- Documents proving assignment of debt to the creditor (contracts, bills of sale, assignment agreements)
- Witness contact info and short statements (neighbors, repair people, previous landlord, anyone who can corroborate timeline or condition)
- Bank statements showing payments or lack of payments
- Utility bills if relevant to habitability or occupancy
- Medical or employment records only if relevant to defenses (bring copies and discuss confidentiality with attorney)
- Court paperwork already filed (summons, complaint, proof of service, notices, prior orders)

How to prepare those items
- Bring originals and 3–5 paper copies (one for the judge, one for opposing counsel, one for you, plus spares), and a clean, indexed digital folder (PDFs named with dates).
- Create a one-page timeline showing key dates (move-in, payments, notices, repair requests, tenant/landlord actions, court filings).
- Label exhibits (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2…) and prepare an exhibit index with short descriptions and dates.
- Prepare short witness summaries (who they are, what they will say, estimated time on direct).

What to do if you can’t gather evidence before the hearing
- Ask attorney whether to request a continuance — grounds could include needing time to subpoena records or secure witnesses.
- Ask attorney to issue subpoenas immediately for time-sensitive records (bank, payment processors, code enforcement).
- Consider getting sworn affidavits from witnesses if they cannot appear live before the next date.
- Ask about filing a motion to compel or preservation letter to stop destruction of evidence.

Practical courtroom tips for the preliminary hearing
- Ask the attorney whether you should appear in person or if they will appear for you.
- If you’ll attend, bring the organized packet, extra copies, and the timeline.
- Be concise and factual when speaking; let your attorney do the legal arguments.
- Note any settlement offers and ask if you should be prepared to discuss terms.

Sample short email you can send to Michael Torres (I can draft and send if you’d like)
