I can help you get organized and make sure you cover the right points with Michael Torres before the preliminary hearing. Below I’ve put together (A) a prioritized list of questions to ask your attorney about whether you need more evidence before the next date, (B) a practical evidence checklist of what to gather, (C) how to organize and present the evidence to the court and to your lawyer, and (D) next steps and an offer to draft an email or call script to Michael Torres.

A. Key questions to ask Michael Torres (prioritized)
- What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing in this case and what will the judge likely decide at that hearing?  
- Given our position and the facts, what specific evidence do you think is necessary to preserve or obtain before the preliminary hearing? Which items are essential vs. optional?  
- Do we need to exchange evidence with the opposing party before the hearing (discovery or pretrial disclosures)? If so, what are the deadlines and format requirements?  
- Are there any procedural steps (motions, requests for continuance, subpoenas, requests for production) you recommend now? If so, who will file them and by when?  
- Should we subpoena any witnesses or documents (e.g., property manager, neighbors, contractors, bank records)? If yes, who and how soon must that be arranged?  
- Is any evidence likely to be excluded (hearsay, unauthenticated texts/photos)? How should I authenticate electronic evidence (texts, emails, photos)?  
- Do we need expert evidence (e.g., property condition or repair estimates) and, if so, when should that be obtained?  
- Could settlement or mediation be beneficial now? Do you recommend negotiating before the preliminary hearing?  
- What should I bring to the preliminary hearing (copies of exhibits, witness list, originals) and how many copies will the court want?  
- If my personal status changed (housing, income, address, employment, contact information, or other relevant status), how will that affect strategy, deadlines, or settlement possibilities?

B. Evidence checklist — items to gather and preserve
Documents (originals if possible; copies and digital backups):
- Lease or rental agreement and any written amendments or addenda.  
- Rent payment records: receipts, money order stubs, canceled checks, bank statements showing payments, PayPal/Venmo records, ledger from landlord.  
- Notices served: eviction notices, pay-or-quit, notices to vacate, any certified mail receipts / proof of service.  
- All communications with landlord/creditor: texts, emails, letters, voicemail transcripts. Save originals and screenshots with timestamps; preserve phones and account records.  
- Repair requests and maintenance logs (dates, photos before/after, invoices, receipts for repairs you paid).  
- Move-in and move-out inspection reports, condition checklists, photos/videos dated near move-in/out.  
- Photographs and videos of property condition, damages, or issues (make sure file metadata or timestamps are preserved).  
- Receipts for expenses related to the property (repairs, cleaning, storage, temporary housing).  
- Police reports or code-violation reports (if any).  
- Any demand letters, collection notices, account statements from the creditor.  
- Court documents and proof of service: summons, complaint, answer, prior filings, notices of hearing.  
- Witness contact info and short written statements/affidavits from neighbors, roommates, contractors, or property managers who observed key events.  
- Any prior judgments, eviction filings, or relevant case history.  
- Identification and proof of residency if applicable.

C. How to organize and present evidence
- Create a one-page timeline summary of key events (dates are crucial). Put dates in chronological order with short notes (e.g., "3/12/25: sent text to landlord about leak; 3/15/25: landlord did not respond").  
- Number exhibits sequentially (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2, etc.) and create an index that lists each exhibit and short description.  
- For the court: bring at least 3 copies of each exhibit (one for judge, one for opposing party, one for your file) unless your attorney advises otherwise. Also bring originals for authenticity. Ask your attorney how many copies the Maricopa County small claims bench typically expects.  
- For electronic evidence: export emails in PDF; take full screenshots of text threads including timestamps and phone numbers. Preserve original files and devices—do not edit or alter them.  
- Prepare brief witness statements (written or sworn affidavits) that your attorney can use or file if needed. Include full contact info and availability.  
- Produce a short "case summary" (1–2 pages) for Michael highlighting the key facts, timeline, and evidence you have and any gaps you’re worried about.

D. Procedural & strategy items to discuss
- Burden of proof and what the plaintiff must show in Maricopa County small claims—ask what their theory is and what we need to counter it.  
- Deadlines for exchanging exhibits, witness lists, and any motion practice.  
- Whether to file any pretrial motions (motion to dismiss, motion in limine) or to ask for continuance to gather evidence.  
- Subpoena procedure/timing and costs (witness subpoenas, records subpoenas).  
- Whether small claims procedures limit formal discovery and what alternatives exist (e.g., subpoenas).  
- Whether evidence (text, photos) must be authenticated through deposition or affidavit for admissibility.  
- Costs and benefits of settlement vs. proceeding to trial, including possible counterclaims and cost recovery.

E. Practical immediate actions (recommended)
- Start compiling the documents above and creating the timeline and exhibit index.  
- Send these materials to Michael at least several days before the hearing (ask him how soon he wants them).  
- Get written witness statements now while memories are fresh.  
- Preserve all electronic evidence (don’t delete texts or emails). Consider taking device backups.  
- Ask Michael whether he wants originals, copies, or PDFs and how many copies for the court.

F. Follow-up — I can help with:
- Drafting an email to Michael Torres listing these questions and attaching a one-page case summary and exhibit index.  
- Creating a printable exhibit index or the one-page timeline from the facts you give me.  
- Drafting brief witness statement templates you can send to your witnesses.

One more thing — you mentioned “my status has changed.” If you want more tailored questions and a plan, please tell me what changed (contact info, address, income, employment, health, incarceration, immigration status, etc.). Some changes could affect scheduling, service, ability to pay, settlement options, or what evidence matters most — I can adapt the checklist and suggested questions accordingly.

