Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and make sure you cover everything with Michael Torres before the preliminary hearing. A few quick clarifying questions before I lay out a checklist:
- When you say your “status has changed,” do you mean your housing situation (moved/evicted), employment/income, or something else that affects the case?
- Is the case in Maricopa County Justice Court / small claims (do you have the court name/number and next hearing date)?
If you want, I can also draft an email to Michael with these questions and the evidence checklist.

Below is a structured list you can use for your meeting or call with Michael — grouped so you can prioritize and keep the conversation focused.

1) Top questions to ask your attorney (high priority)
- Based on my changed status, do I need additional evidence now or before the next hearing? What specifically would matter most?
- What is the exact purpose of the preliminary hearing in this court (what will the judge decide)? What should I be prepared to present at that hearing?
- What is the court’s timeline for exchanging exhibits or evidence? Are there deadlines to file anything before the hearing?
- Do we need to file any motions before the preliminary hearing (e.g., continuance, motion to dismiss, motion for more definite statement)?
- Are we ready to go to trial if it’s not resolved at the preliminary hearing? If not, can we request a continuance and what is our likelihood of getting one?
- What defenses or counterclaims should we raise (improper notice, failure to follow eviction procedure, habitability, rent paid, setoff, etc.)?
- What standard of proof does the plaintiff need to meet and what is our burden for affirmative defenses or counterclaims?
- If we don’t have a needed witness or document, should we subpoena them now? How do we get subpoenas issued in this court and how long does it take?
- If the creditor is awarded a judgment, what are the practical consequences (wage garnishment, bank levy, collections, eviction steps)? How does my changed status affect enforcement or settlement options?

2) Evidence checklist (bring these items to your meeting and hearing)
Critical (gives best protection / must-haves)
- Copy of complaint, summons, and any court filings you’ve received
- Lease or rental agreement (signed copies)
- Any written notices (pay or quit notices, termination notices) — originals and proof of delivery (certified mail receipts, hand-delivery receipts)
- Rent payment records (rent ledger, receipts, bank statements, cancelled checks showing payments)
- All communications with landlord/creditor (texts, emails, letters) — preserved and organized chronologically
- Move-in / move-out inspection reports, condition photos (dates visible)
- Repair requests and responses (work orders, repair receipts)
- Police reports, code inspection reports, or city citations related to habitability or disturbances (if relevant)
- Any written settlement offers or negotiations

Helpful (strengthen your case)
- Photographs or videos with timestamps (conditions, damage)
- Witness contact information and short written statements/affidavits (neighbors, roommates, vendors)
- Receipts for expenses or repairs you paid (if asserting setoff)
- Copies of demand letters you sent or received
- Prior judgments or disputes with this landlord/creditor (if relevant)

Optional (useful depending on your defenses)
- Medical records (if habitability affected health) — be cautious about sensitive info
- Proof of alternate housing or attempts to mitigate
- Relevant utility records

3) Questions about witnesses and subpoenas
- Which witnesses are essential and which are merely helpful?
- If a witness is reluctant, can you subpoena them now? What’s the timeline and cost?
- Do we need written affidavits from witnesses in advance? Does the court accept sworn statements by email?

4) Court procedure & logistics to confirm
- How many copies of exhibits does the court require and do we need to provide them to the opposing side beforehand?
- Do exhibits need to be pre-marked or filed with the court before the hearing/trial?
- Time limits for testimony at hearing/trial and how exhibits are admitted
- Whether remote (phone/video) appearances are allowed and how to request them
- Location and check-in procedures for the hearing (arrive early, bring photo ID, clerk’s counter info)

5) Strategic / settlement questions
- Do we have leverage to settle now? If so, what settlement options make sense given my changed status?
- Should we make any settlement offer before the hearing, and what terms should be avoided?
- Is mediation available or likely to be ordered in this type of case?

6) Trial readiness and evidentiary issues
- How will we authenticate documents (bank statements, texts)? Do we need metadata or certified copies?
- If the plaintiff relies on an accounting or ledger, can we demand supporting documentation?
- Are there any hearsay concerns for witness statements or documents? How should we prepare to lay foundation?
- If the court excludes certain evidence, what is plan B?

7) Tactical, immediate steps you can take now (before speaking to Michael or before the hearing)
- Create a chronological timeline of events with dates and short descriptions (one page).
- Gather and make 3 sets of organized exhibits: one for the judge, one for opposing counsel, one for you.
- Ask key witnesses for brief written statements and contact info; get them ready to sign affidavits if needed.
- Scan and back up all documents and communications; label digital files by date.
- If you need a subpoena, ask your attorney to request it immediately (subpoena lead times vary).
- If you’re short on evidence, ask Michael about requesting a continuance—do it early.
- If your status change affects income, housing, or ability to comply, get documentation (pay stubs, benefit letters) to corroborate your situation.

8) Practical questions about outcomes and enforcement
- If plaintiff wins, how will judgment be enforced in Maricopa County (garnishment, bank levy, execution)? Are there exemptions available?
- How long until a judgment becomes enforceable? Are there stays or appeals options?
- What are the costs/fees we might incur if we go to trial vs. if we settle?

9) How to prioritize evidence if time is short
- Priority 1: Lease, notices, payment records, court papers, communications proving payments or status.
- Priority 2: Photos, repair/condition records, witness statements.
- Priority 3: Secondary receipts, estimates, extra photos.

10) Suggested sample phrasing to ask your attorney
- “Given that my [explain status change briefly], what additional evidence would you recommend we collect before the preliminary hearing to protect my defenses or counterclaims?”
- “Do you think we should subpoena [name] now? How long will that take?”
- “If we aren’t ready, can we successfully request a continuance, and what reasons should we cite?”
- “What specific exhibits would you like me to bring to the hearing, and how many copies?”

Next step — I can:
- Draft a short email to Michael Torres listing these questions and asking for specific guidance (I can include your court date and status change if you provide them).
- Turn the checklist into a one-page printable hearing packet you can bring to court.
Tell me which you prefer and give me any extra details (hearing date, what you mean by status change, Michael’s email) and I’ll draft the email or packet for you.

