I can help — this is a good time to get a focused checklist so you and Michael Torres can decide quickly whether to collect more evidence. Before that, quick question: when you say your “status has changed,” what changed exactly (employment/income, residence, health, bankruptcy, incarceration, immigration status, contact info, etc.)? That will change which evidence matters.

Below is a clear, prioritized set of things to ask your attorney, what evidence to gather, how to organize it, and next steps to be ready for the preliminary hearing and potential trial.

1) Top questions to ask Michael Torres (prioritize these at the start of the conversation)
- What is our legal strategy for the preliminary hearing and possible trial? (defense only, counterclaim, settlement posture)
- Based on what I’ve already provided, do we need additional evidence before the next court date? If so, what specifically?
- What is the most important evidence to have at the preliminary hearing vs. at trial?
- Are there any immediate deadlines or discovery tasks I must complete before the hearing?
- Do you recommend subpoenas for documents or witnesses? If so, who and by when?
- Will we need originals or are copies acceptable? Should we bring electronic versions?
- Are any of my communications (texts, emails, voice messages) likely to be admissible? Any authentication needed?
- If my “status changed” (explain your change), how does that affect damages, ability to pay, or defenses?
- Do we need expert testimony or an inspector (e.g., for habitability/damage claims)?
- Should we prepare witness affidavits or in-court witness testimony? How many witnesses is reasonable?
- What is your recommended settlement range and strategy (if settlement is an option)?
- How long before the hearing do you want all documents and a final exhibit list?
- What should I expect at the preliminary hearing (possible outcomes: continuance, settlement, scheduling, small hearing on evidence)?
- What are the likely next steps if the judge sets a trial? What timeline and prep will that require?
- What are the cost implications for subpoenas, experts, or depositions?

2) Evidence & documents checklist — collect and prioritize
High priority (bring to attorney immediately):
- Lease/rental agreement or written occupancy agreement
- All notices and court documents you received (eviction notice, complaint, summons, proof of service)
- Payment records: bank statements, cancelled checks, money order receipts, rent payment ledger, Venmo/PayPal/Zelle transactions (with timestamps)
- All communications with landlord/creditor: emails, texts, letters, voicemails (save originals; do not delete)
- Receipts for repairs, rent, utilities paid on your behalf, or other expense receipts tied to tenancy
- Photos/videos of the property condition with timestamps and location info
- Move-in/move-out condition reports, inspection forms, and security-deposit correspondence
- Any roommate or co-tenant agreements or who paid what

Medium priority:
- Witness contact info and short witness statements (neighbors, witnesses to payments, landlord’s behavior)
- Utility bills showing occupancy dates
- Notices you mailed (certified mail receipts) or received (return receipts)
- Previous court decisions or settlement agreements (if any) with this party

Lower priority but possibly useful:
- Medical notes or evidence if habitability affected health
- Police reports, code-enforcement complaints, repair requests, or city inspection reports
- Bank/creditor correspondence showing debt assignment or collection attempts
- Employment/income records (if disputing claimed damages or ability to pay)

3) How to organize evidence (make this for Michael)
- Create a one-page timeline of events (dates and one-line description) — this is the single best tool to explain your case quickly.
- Number exhibits (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2, etc.). Create an index with short descriptions.
- Provide 3 copies of exhibit packet: one for the judge/court (if required), one for opposing party, one for your file/attorney. Also prepare a labeled digital folder (PDFs) you can email.
- Highlight or annotate key lines in documents (payments, dates, signatures).
- Prepare short witness statements (email or one-page typed) and include contact info and best times to reach them.

4) Questions about admissibility & preservation
- Ask whether original documents are required or if certified bank records are needed.
- Ask which forms of electronic communication the court accepts as evidence and how to authenticate them.
- Confirm you should not delete any messages, emails, or voicemails — preserve everything and back it up.
- If evidence was lost or destroyed, ask how to document that and whether an affidavit will help.

5) Procedural and tactical items to confirm
- Confirm the exact date/time/location of the preliminary hearing and who will appear (you, lawyer, both).
- Ask whether Michael will appear or wants you to appear and what you should say/do if you must speak.
- Ask what the judge typically does at preliminary hearings and whether settlement conferences or mediation are likely.
- Ask whether we should file any motions (continuance, motion to dismiss, motion to compel documents) before the hearing.
- If subpoenas are needed, ask who will prepare/serve them and the estimated timeline/cost.

6) If it likely goes to trial — what to prepare now
- Finalize exhibit list; get originals or certified copies where needed.
- Line up witnesses and confirm their availability on possible trial dates.
- Prepare and rehearse direct-examination questions; get written witness statements signed.
- Determine whether demonstrative evidence (photos/diagrams) would help and prepare those with clear labels.
- Discuss settlement thresholds and whether you should attempt a pretrial settlement.

7) Practical timeline recommendations (example)
- Immediately (today–48 hours): preserve all electronic communications, make copies/backups of everything, start timeline.
- Within 3–7 days: send current document packet and timeline to Michael for review.
- 7–14 days before hearing: finalize exhibit list and identify subpoena needs.
- 3–5 days before hearing: deliver final exhibits to Michael and confirm who will appear.

8) Suggested short message script/email to Michael (you can copy/paste)
