I can help — below is a focused set of questions and a practical evidence checklist you can use with Michael Torres (Dowd Legal) to determine whether you need to gather more evidence before the next date. If you want, I can turn this into a short email or call script to send to him.

First: one quick clarifying question
- When you say your “status has changed,” what changed? (e.g., income, housing, employment, health, or something else?) That can affect what evidence is important.

What to ask your attorney (grouped & prioritized)
- Case status & next steps
  - What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing in our case? (Is it scheduling, motions, or trial-ready?)
  - What outcome should I expect at that hearing and what will trigger a trial?
  - Are there any court deadlines (discovery, exhibit lists, witness lists) I must meet before the next hearing?
- Evidence & admissibility
  - Based on our defenses, what specific evidence do you think is essential to present at the next hearing?
  - Which of my documents are likely to be admissible and which might be excluded (hearsay, authentication issues)?
  - Do you recommend affidavits or sworn witness statements in advance?
- Witnesses & subpoenas
  - Do we need witnesses, and if so, who (previous tenants, neighbors, maintenance workers, property manager)?
  - Should I get written witness statements now or try to subpoena witnesses for the hearing/trial?
- Document collection & organization
  - How many copies of exhibits should I bring? Do you want physical copies, electronic, or both?
  - How should we label and number exhibits? Do you want an exhibit list prepared in a specific format?
- Service & proof
  - Do we have proof of proper service of notices and filings? If not, how should I obtain and preserve that proof (certified mail receipts, process server, sheriff returns)?
- Discovery & evidence preservation
  - Are there discovery requests I should expect or serve now (requests for documents, admissions, interrogatories)?
  - What steps should I take immediately to preserve evidence and communications (emails, texts, voicemail, photos, repair receipts)?
- Strategy & defenses
  - Based on the landlord’s claims, what defenses or counterclaims should I prepare evidence for (nonpayment, habitability, retaliation, wrongful eviction, security deposit dispute)?
  - Would filing any pre-hearing motions be helpful (motion to dismiss, motion to strike evidence)?
- Expert testimony & reports
  - Do we need an expert (housing inspector, contractor) and if so, can we obtain a short inspection report before the hearing?
- Settlement/mediation
  - Is the court open to mediation or settlement before the preliminary hearing or trial? Would you recommend attempting settlement now?
  - If settlement is possible, what information or documentation should I prepare to negotiate?
- Logistics & consequences
  - If we are not ready at the preliminary hearing, what are the consequences (continuance, default, sanctions)?
  - What should I bring to the hearing (photo ID, original documents, copies, witnesses)?
- Costs & fees
  - Will additional evidence collection (inspections, subpoenas) incur additional cost? What is the likely cost and who pays court costs if we win?

Evidence checklist — gather these ASAP (prioritize 1–6 if short on time)
1. Lease & tenancy documents
   - Signed lease or rental agreement, amendments, copy of move-in/out forms.
2. Notices & correspondence
   - Any eviction notices, pay-or-quit notices, breach notices; certified mail receipts or proof of service.
   - All communications with landlord/creditor: emails, texts, calls (save originals and screenshots with dates).
3. Payment records
   - Rent ledger, receipts, cancelled checks, bank statements, PayPal/Zelle records showing payments and dates.
4. Photos & video
   - Dated photos/videos of unit condition, damages, repairs, pests, habitability issues — include metadata or a dated log.
5. Repair/maintenance records
   - Work orders, receipts, contractor invoices, inspection reports, requests made to landlord and any responses.
6. Witness info & statements
   - Names, contact info, short written statements or affidavits from neighbors, contractors, or anyone who can corroborate facts.
7. Court & service paperwork
   - Copies of summons, complaint, proof of service, prior court filings, and any rulings/orders already issued.
8. Official documents relevant to your changed status
   - If your “status change” is income or housing-related, bring pay stubs, termination notices, benefits letters, or new lease/move documentation.
9. Timeline and summary
   - A one-page chronological timeline (dates, short descriptions) of key events — very helpful to give to your attorney and the judge.
10. Anything that undermines plaintiff’s claim
   - Documents showing landlord/creditor accepted late payments, made contradictory statements, or failed to follow local eviction procedures.

How to organize the evidence for your attorney and the court
- Create an exhibit binder (or a numbered PDF folder): each item labeled Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2, etc., with a cover sheet listing contents.
- Prepare 2–3 copies: one for the judge, one for opposing counsel, one for yourself (plus an electronic copy on a USB).
- Provide your attorney with a short witness list and exhibit list at least several days before the hearing.

Practical preservation tips
- Don’t delete texts, emails, or voicemails related to the case — export/save them as PDFs and make backups.
- Timestamp photos or keep originals on your phone/computer; note when they were taken.
- Get written statements or affidavits from witnesses ASAP while their recollection is fresh.
- If documents are only with the landlord (e.g., rent ledger), ask your attorney about subpoenas to compel production.

Questions specific to Maricopa County small claims / local practice
- Ask Michael: Are there any Maricopa County local rules or forms we must file before the preliminary hearing?
- Is the hearing held remotely or in person? (If remote, confirm the video link and technical requirements.)
- Ask if the court uses any special exhibit submission procedures or deadlines.

Next actions I can take for you
- Turn this into a concise email to Michael Torres or a short call-script — I can draft it now.
- Create an organized exhibit list template or a one-page timeline for you to fill in.
- If you want, tell me the date of the next hearing and what changed in your status and I’ll customize the checklist and questions further.

Would you like me to draft the email to Michael Torres with these questions and the evidence list? If so, tell me whether you want it short (bullet questions) or detailed (includes checklist and requests).

