Assistant: I can help you get organized and prepared for the preliminary hearing and possible trial. Below I’ve put together a focused set of questions to ask Michael Torres, a practical evidence checklist, prioritized next steps for before the next court date, and a short email template you can send your lawyer. If you want, I can draft a longer email you can send or help organize the documents into numbered exhibits.

1) High-level goals for your conversation with Michael
- Confirm the current status and what the preliminary hearing will decide.  
- Understand what evidence is needed now vs. what can be developed later.  
- Identify deadlines (evidence exchange/filings, subpoenas, witness lists, motions).  
- Agree on who will do what (you collect documents, lawyer files motions, etc.).

2) Questions to ask Michael Torres (organized by topic)
Case status & timeline
- What exactly will be addressed at the preliminary hearing? Is it scheduling, a pretrial conference, or will the court take evidence?
- What are the key deadlines between now and trial (discovery, exhibit exchange, filing motions, settlement conference)?
- How likely is the case to settle before trial? Would you recommend attempting settlement/mediation now?

Evidence & exhibits
- Based on our facts, do we currently have enough evidence to meet our objectives at the preliminary hearing and to be in good shape for trial?
- Which pieces of evidence are most important for our defense/response (lease, notices, receipts, communications, photos)?
- Are there any additional records I should try to obtain (bank statements, certified mail receipts, repair invoices, inspection reports)?
- Do photos and videos need metadata/timestamps or an affidavit to be admissible?

Witnesses & statements
- Which witnesses (neighbors, contractors, former tenants, property manager) would strengthen our case?
- Should we get written witness statements or affidavits now? Will they be admitted at preliminary hearing?
- How do we subpoena someone if necessary, and what’s the timeline to issue subpoenas in Maricopa County?

Document handling & authentication
- How should I label and organize documents/exhibits? How many copies should I bring to court?
- Do we need certified copies of any documents (e.g., lease, judgment, prior filings)?
- Will signed declarations under penalty of perjury work in lieu of live witness testimony for some things?

Procedures & motions
- Are there any motions we should file before the preliminary hearing (motion to dismiss, motion for continuance, motion to compel discovery)?
- Does the creditor have to exchange evidence with us prior to the hearing? What are the rules in small claims/justice court here?
- Are there local Maricopa County procedures or forms I should be aware of?

Trial readiness & strategy
- If the case goes to trial, what is our plan for presenting evidence (order of witnesses, demonstrative exhibits, key themes)?
- How long will our presentation likely be at trial and are there time limits in small claims?
- What are the strengths and weaknesses of our position from your perspective?

Practical/logistics
- What should I bring to the preliminary hearing (original documents, copies, ID, timeline of events)?
- How should I behave in court and what will happen procedurally that day?
- Who will speak at the hearing — you or should I be prepared to speak?

3) Evidence checklist — documents and items to gather
Essential documents
- Lease or rental agreement (full document, any amendments).  
- Notices: notice to vacate, pay-or-quit, demand letters, certified mail receipts.  
- The complaint/summons and any court filings from the creditor.  
- Proof of payments and rent ledger (bank statements, canceled checks, money order receipts, Venmo/PayPal screenshots showing full details).  
- Security deposit documentation and communications about return.  
- Communications: all texts, emails, written messages, maintenance requests, landlord replies. Save originals/screenshots and create a timeline.

Condition & habitability evidence (if relevant)
- Photos/videos of unit condition with dates (include timestamps or metadata if possible).  
- Repair invoices, receipts, inspection reports, contractor estimates.  
- Any code violation or municipal inspection reports, police reports (if incidents occurred).

Third-party evidence
- Witness names and contact info (neighbors, contractors, property manager).  
- Written witness statements/affidavits signed and dated if witnesses can’t attend.

Court & service documents
- Proof of service of process documents, certified mail tracking, affidavits of service.  
- Any prior judgments, small claims history, or related court orders.

Other supporting documents
- Moving/move-in condition checklist, inventory, photographs from move-in/move-out.  
- Any communications from property management company.  
- Records of attempts to resolve (mediation calls, settlement offers).

4) Prioritized “do now” list before your next court date
1. Confirm the date and time of the preliminary hearing and tell your lawyer if that date conflicts with anything.  
2. Send Michael a concise timeline (dates + short note) of the key events and attach the documents above that you already have. Ask which of those he needs immediately.  
3. Collect top five items: lease, complaint/summons, payment proof, key communications (texts/emails), and photos of the unit. Make clear copies and keep originals.  
4. Make a witness list with contact info and short notes on what each can testify to. Ask your lawyer which ones to secure affidavits from now.  
5. Ask about subpoenas immediately if witnesses or records (bank, property manager) are critical and might be hard to get.

5) How to collect and preserve evidence effectively
- Save original digital files and make backups (cloud + external drive).  
- For texts/screenshots: capture full threads, show sender and timestamps, and export or print to PDF.  
- For photos/videos: keep original files (with metadata). If only phone photos, don’t edit; include a short note describing when/how they were taken.  
- For receipts/bank records: get printed/official copies when possible.  
- For witness statements: ask witnesses to sign and date a short written statement or affidavit. Note contact details and relationship to the case.

6) Short phrases to ask your lawyer (ready-to-send or to say on a call)
- “Based on the complaint and our documents, do you think we need additional evidence before the preliminary hearing? If so, which specific pieces?”  
- “What are the deadlines for exchanging evidence or filing motions in Maricopa County Justice Court for this case?”  
- “Which witnesses should I secure affidavits from now, and who should we plan to subpoena?”  
- “Do photos and videos need metadata or an affidavit to be admitted? If yes, what format do you prefer?”  
- “Can you summarize the likely issues the judge will consider at the preliminary hearing and what outcome we should prepare for?”

7) Sample email template to Michael Torres
