I can help you get organized and make the most of your next meeting/hearing. Below is a concise, prioritized checklist of things to do and specific questions to ask Michael Torres so you can determine whether you need more evidence before the next date.

First — quick clarifying questions for you
- When you say your “status has changed,” what do you mean (change of address, employment/income, tenancy status, moved out, health, incarceration, bankruptcy, etc.)?
- What is the exact court date coming up (preliminary hearing date)? How many days until it?
- Is this matter in Justice Court (eviction/forcible detainer) or small claims/limited civil? (You said small claims & eviction — those are different tracks; I can tailor questions if you confirm.)
- Do you want me to draft an email to Michael with these questions?

Checklist: immediate actions (high priority)
1. Give these facts/documents to Michael now:
   - Court paperwork you’ve received (complaint/summons, any motions from opposing party, proof of service).
   - Lease agreement or written rental terms (if applicable).
   - Notices (pay or quit, eviction notices, demand letters).
   - Proofs of payment or nonpayment: rent checks, bank transfers, receipts, ledger.
   - Communications with the creditor/landlord: emails, texts, letters, voicemail transcripts.
   - Photos/videos (dates visible if possible) of the property condition, damages, repairs.
   - Repair requests and landlord responses, invoices, estimates.
   - Any prior court judgments or collection paperwork related to this debt.
   - Your calendar/notes of events (move-out date, conversations, dates you were served).
   - ID and contact info for potential witnesses.

2. Preserve evidence now:
   - Save and back up all electronic communications and photos.
   - Keep originals of checks/receipts; get copies if you gave originals away.
   - Document the chain of events in chronological bullets (one page timeline).

Questions to ask Michael Torres (grouped & ready to use)
A. Case status & strategy
- What is our current status in court and what is realistically likely to happen at the preliminary hearing?
- What is your recommended goal for that hearing (settlement, continuance, scheduling a trial, dismissal)?
- Given my changed status, does that change our strategy or urgency?

B. Evidence & proof
- Based on what you’ve seen so far, what specific pieces of evidence are missing that could materially affect the outcome?
- Which documents should I prioritize gathering before the hearing (top 5)?
- Are there particular types of evidence the judge will expect to see at preliminary vs. trial?
- Do we need notarized affidavits from witnesses, and if so, who should provide them?

C. Witnesses & subpoenas
- Do we need live witnesses at the hearing or trial? Which ones are most important?
- If a witness is uncooperative, can we subpoena them now? What’s the timeline?
- Do you want written witness statements or affidavits in advance?

D. Discovery & motions
- Is informal discovery allowed here (exchange of documents)? If so, what’s the deadline?
- Should we file any pretrial motions now (motion to continue, motion to dismiss, motion for more time to prepare)?
- If I need more time to collect evidence, what’s the process and likelihood of getting a continuance?

E. Settlement & negotiation
- Is settlement negotiations appropriate before the preliminary hearing? Are you open to mediation?
- What settlement terms are reasonable to consider (payment plans, dismissal upon payment, reduced amount)?
- If I settle, what impact does that have on my credit or ability to stay in the home (if eviction involved)?

F. Consequences & collateral issues
- If we lose or there’s a judgment against me, what are the likely consequences (eviction, wage garnishment, bank levy, credit impact in Maricopa County/Arizona)?
- Does my changed status (describe) create any immediate legal risks (e.g., bankruptcy implications, loss of right to counsel, inability to attend hearings)?
- Are there remedies or defenses specific to landlord-tenant or creditor claims I should consider (e.g., improper service, statute of limitations, rent offset, improper notice)?

G. Logistics & deadlines
- What are the upcoming court deadlines I must not miss?
- Where/when is the next hearing and will you attend or do I need to appear? If you attend, will you need anything from me beforehand?
- What’s the timeline to submit exhibits/witness lists to the court (if required)?

H. Costs & next steps
- What additional costs (filing fees, subpoena fees, investigator fees, expert witness) might we incur if we proceed to trial?
- What do you recommend I do between now and the next hearing (top 3 tasks)?

Practical evidence checklist to bring/collect before the next date
- Court papers (complaint/summons, any responses you filed)
- Lease and any amendments
- All rent payment proof: bank statements, canceled checks, money order receipts
- Communications (printouts of emails, texts, messages, certified mail receipts)
- Photos/videos with dates
- Repair invoices, receipts, and any estimates
- Move-in/move-out checklist or inspection forms
- Copies of any previous judgments or collection notices
- Names & contact info of witnesses plus short summaries of what each will say
- Any police or code enforcement reports related to the property

How to prioritize the evidence
- Urgent (collect now): court papers, payment proof, communication logs, proof of service, time-sensitive witness availability.
- Important before preliminary hearing: photos, text threads, witnesses’ short affidavits.
- If trial is set later: get formal witness affidavits, subpoenaed documents, expert reports.

If you need, I can:
- Draft a concise email/message to Michael that lists these questions and requests the next steps.
- Create a one-page timeline template for you to fill in and share with your lawyer.
- Help sort your documents into a list you can send to Michael.

Next step for me
Tell me:
- What exactly you meant by “status has changed”
- The next hearing date and whether you currently plan to attend with Michael in court
- If you want me to draft the email to Michael now (and whether you want it short or detailed)

Once you confirm, I’ll draft the email or timeline for you.

