I can help you get organized and ready for the preliminary hearing. Below I’ve put together (A) a focused list of questions to ask Michael Torres, (B) a prioritized evidence checklist to collect before the next date, (C) practical steps to take now, and (D) a couple quick follow-ups I need from you so I can help further (and an offer to draft an email to your lawyer if you want).

Note: I’m not your lawyer — treat these as organizational/legal-preparation suggestions and run final decisions/strategy by Michael.

A. Key questions to ask Michael Torres (use these at your next meeting or call)
- What is the specific purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing in our case, and what standard or issues will the judge address then?
- Based on our position and the opposing party’s claims, do you expect this case to settle at/after the preliminary hearing or to proceed to trial?
- Do we need to produce any documents or disclosures to the court or opposing party before the hearing? Are there deadlines?
- What evidence would be most persuasive to the judge at the preliminary hearing vs. at trial?
- Do you recommend getting additional evidence now (witness statements, expert estimates, bank records, etc.)? If so, which items should be prioritized?
- Are there any motions we should file before the preliminary hearing (motion to dismiss, strike, compel, protective motion)?
- Do we need signed affidavits now or will unsworn witness statements be enough until trial?
- Are there service or jurisdiction issues, statute-of-limitations, or procedural defects we should pursue as defenses?
- If the matter goes to trial, what will you need from me (number of exhibits, witness availability, originals vs. copies)?
- Should I subpoena records (bank statements, phone records, property manager records) now? If yes, which ones and how long will subpoenas take?
- What is the likely outcome if we lose (money judgment, eviction, credit reporting)? What are our exposure limits and post-judgment options?
- Are there local Maricopa County small-claims/justice-court practices or judge preferences we should know about?
- What is your recommended settlement threshold (minimum acceptable offer / walk-away point)?
- Are there specific ways I should authenticate texts/emails/photos for court (metadata, screenshots with timestamps, notarized affidavits)?

B. Evidence and documents to gather (prioritize collecting these before the hearing)
- Basic case documents:
  - Complaint/claim, summons, any court filings, and any documents the creditor filed or served
  - Your answer/counterclaim (if filed) and any prior court notices
- Lease and tenancy:
  - Signed lease or rental agreement (all pages)
  - Any amendments or written rent agreements
- Payment records:
  - Bank statements, cleared checks, money order receipts, PayPal/online payment records showing rent payments or partial payments
  - A rent ledger (chronological list of payments and balances)
- Notices and communications:
  - All written notices (pay-or-quit, breach notices, termination notices) and proof of receipt (certified mail receipts, signed returns)
  - All emails, texts, messages, chat logs with landlord/management/creditor (export/save them, include metadata if possible)
- Repairs/habitability/issues:
  - Repair requests, emails to landlord, maintenance tickets, inspection reports, photos/videos showing condition, dates and timestamps
  - Contractor invoices, estimates, receipts for repairs you paid
- Evidence of service:
  - Proof of how you (or they) were served with court papers
- Witnesses:
  - Names and contact info of witnesses (neighbors, former tenants, property manager, contractors). Short affidavit drafts or written statements signed/dated if possible.
- Photographic/video evidence:
  - Clear dated photos/videos with context (file metadata if available). Consider adding a short caption noting date/time/location for each file.
- Police or incident reports:
  - Any police reports or municipal reports relevant to the property or disputes
- Communications about payments/offers/agreements:
  - Any text/email where the creditor/landlord agreed to concessions, payment plans, or acknowledged issues
- Credit/financial records (if relevant to defenses or damages):
  - Documents showing damages, costs you incurred, receipts for relocation, storage, etc.
- Anything that undermines plaintiff’s claim:
  - Documents showing tenant was not notified properly, paid rent, or landlord breached obligations

C. Practical preparation & courtroom logistics
- Copies: Make at least three sets of organized exhibits (one for judge, one for opposing counsel, one for you). Label exhibits (Exhibit 1, 2, …) and include a one-page exhibit list.
- Originals vs. copies: Ask Michael which originals he needs; bring originals and an extra set of copies.
- Witnesses: Confirm who must appear at the preliminary hearing versus the trial. If a witness can’t attend, consider a signed affidavit or subpoena.
- Authentication: Ask Michael how he prefers you authenticate texts/photos (print with metadata, include affidavits).
- Deadlines: Confirm any discovery/exhibit exchange deadlines and deadlines to file motions or witness lists.
- Subpoenas: If records are needed from third parties (banks, phone companies, property managers), ask about issuing subpoenas now — they take time.
- Settlement: Consider whether you authorize Michael to negotiate/accept a settlement up to a certain amount, or if he must call you first.
- Court day checklist: ID, copies of exhibits, notebook with timeline, witness contact numbers, any receipts.

D. Prioritized immediate action items (what to do this week)
1. Tell me the date of the preliminary hearing (so I can help map timelines).
2. Gather and make digital backups of lease, payment records, notices, and all communications (emails/texts). Export/texts to a PDF if possible.
3. Draft a concise timeline (one-page): move-in date, missed payments (if any), key communications, notices, court filing dates.
4. Identify 1–3 witnesses and get short signed statements if possible.
5. Ask Michael about subpoenas and object/document deadlines — do this ASAP if records are needed.
6. Prepare exhibit copies and a one-page exhibit list; leave time to finalize after you talk to Michael.

E. A few clarifying questions for you
- What exactly changed about your status? (e.g., moved out, lost income, signed a new agreement, received a notice, changed address)
- What is the date of the preliminary hearing?
- Have you already filed an answer or engaged Michael to appear on your behalf (i.e., is he already on file with the court)?
- Do you already have most of the documents above, or do you need help organizing/creating the timeline and exhibit list?

